rnal","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"05b47bfa-ca23-4ead-b41e-c6254aa4a2cc","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d50c13ce-05db-402d-a20c-8f3393d035f7","agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WmsiGnBhcRftDXxc2Bfu9T","type":"tool_result","content":"######## claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: impulse and momentum\ncategory: [biomechanics, force-time-curve, testing]\nconfidence: proven\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Newton's Second Law and the Impulse-Momentum Relation as the Mathematical Base of the Force-Time Curve, With the Book's Loose Wording Marked\n\n**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 5 by saying that if \"the Force-Time curve is to be regarded as the graphical starting point for sport biomechanics, then Newton's 2nd Law of Motion must logically serve as the corresponding mathematical starting point\" (PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They state Newton II as \"Force is proportional to the rate of change of momentum,\" write it as equation (5.1), F = d(m.v)/dt = m.dv/dt + v.dm/dt, and reduce it to F = m.a \"if the mass of the system is not changing with time,\" which they say holds in sport (PDF p. 266-267 / printed p. 255-256; equations verified against the page image). They then separate force \"generated ballistically or impulsively\" from force \"produced more slowly\": for slow force \"it is useful to refer to the momentum m.v of the body,\" but for \"brief transient force development, it is conventional to talk about the impulse I.\" The impulse is written I = ∫F.dt = ∫m.dv, or I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV, and \"Even if neither F nor ΔT is known, the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256, verified against the page image). Scope of `proven`: only the identity that the impulse of the net force on a body of constant mass equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv). The sentences listed in When NOT are outside that scope.\n\n**Why it works** — Inference: this is textbook mechanics. Integrating F_net = m.dv/dt over time gives ∫F_net dt = mΔv, so the area under a net-force-time curve equals the change in momentum whatever the curve's shape. That is why the take-off velocity of a jump fixes the net impulse even when the force record is not known, which is the authors' \"even if neither F nor ΔT is known\" point. The authors give no further mechanism; they use the relation to frame the RFD comparison of two athletes repeated from Ch 2.2.2 (Fig 5.1).\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"Force plates (CMJ testing) were already the department's longitudinal performance-tracking tool\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 73), and force-plate metrics are chosen by the ODSF framework, with \"1-3 driver metrics (what produces the output, e.g. braking/propulsive force)\" (line 79, adopted vendor framework). Inference: the impulse-momentum identity is what links a driver metric such as propulsive impulse to the output metric (take-off velocity, hence jump height), so it is the right lens when a jump changes and staff ask whether the athlete produced more impulse or the same impulse in a different way. Use the authors' split as a reading rule: for brief ballistic actions (take-off, throw release, strike) think in impulse over the short contact time; for slow efforts, momentum and peak force are adequate.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Four sentences in the passage are loose and should not be quoted as physics. (a) Inference: the v.dm/dt term in equation (5.1) is not a valid general form for a system that loses mass (the correct form uses the velocity of the ejected mass relative to the body), though the book's own conclusion F = m.a for constant mass, the sport case, is right. (b) Inference: \"I = F.ΔT\" is true only if F is read as the average force over ΔT; the book does not say so. (c) The authors write that the area under athlete B's curve in Fig 5.1 is \"(i.e. the momentum)\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: the area under a force-time curve is that force's impulse; it equals a body's change in momentum only when it is the net force on a free body. In Fig 5.1 the athletes perform a maximal effort and \"hold the load until fatigue forced them to stop,\" so no body is gaining that momentum; read the area as total impulse of effort, not momentum. (d) The authors write that \"any change in direction, and not only magnitude, will also be associated with a change in force\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: any change in the velocity vector, direction alone included, requires a net force; whether the force itself changes depends on the motion (constant-speed circular motion has a constant-size force that only turns). Do not use (c) or (d) as stated.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is Fig 5.1 (verified against the page image): athlete A exerts more force than B at any instant before time T2, so A has the advantage where \"rapid RFD\" is needed (jumping, striking, throwing), while B reaches the higher peak and sustains force longer, an advantage in \"wrestling, powerlifting and scrummaging in rugby\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). This comparison is already atomized from Ch 2.2.2 in [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]].\n\n**Related** — [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]] (Fig 5.1 repeats the Ch 2 two-athlete comparison); [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]] (4.3.4, the authors' caution on comparing impulse across movements); [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]] (4.3.4, the companion warning on power); [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]]\n######## principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: independence of combined motor qualities\ncategory: [motor-abilities, speed-strength, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Speed-Strength, Strength-Endurance and Speed-Endurance Are Independent Qualities, Not By-Products of Strength, Speed and Endurance\n\n**What it is** — The authors state that \"speed-strength, strength-endurance and speed-endurance are not simply derivatives of strength, speed and endurance, but are totally independent qualities.\" Their evidence is that \"an increase in absolute strength does not necessarily enhance any of these three qualities.\" So the combined qualities \"should be placed alongside the latter in a group, each with its own particular means and methods of development (Verkhoshansky, 1963)\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257). The same paragraph frames force and velocity as qualities that, \"at a high level of development are inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4),\" and says excessive development of both \"is not required\" because they are \"interrelated aspects\" of all motor activity; depending on the movement, \"one of these qualities achieves greater development\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism beyond the observation that absolute strength gains do not carry over automatically. Inference: \"inversely proportional\" here is the authors' loose wording about trained motor qualities, not a statement of Hill's force-velocity equation, and should not be read as a law of mechanics.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Vermeil hierarchy (work capacity, strength, power, speed, sport skill, \"each level a required foundation for the next\") is the department's programming \"compass\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101, Explicit, Q1). Inference: this principle refines that compass rather than contradicting it: strength can be a foundation for power and speed, but the authors say a strength gain does not by itself buy speed-strength or strength-endurance, so each combined quality needs its own block of means. Use it when a strong athlete's jump, sprint or repeat-effort numbers stall while the squat keeps rising.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The claim is \"does not necessarily enhance,\" not \"never enhances.\" Elsewhere the book records that qualities transfer more in the early stages of training ([[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]), so for novices a general strength gain may still lift the combined qualities. The authors cite one source (Verkhoshansky 1963) and give no data on this page. Inference: do not use the \"inversely proportional\" sentence to argue that strength training slows athletes; the book's own Ch 2-4 material on the strength-speed continuum is the place for that question.\n\n**Example** — Inference: no example is given on this page. A lineman whose back squat rises over an off-season block while his 10-yard split and repeat-sprint times do not change fits the authors' claim that absolute strength gains need not carry into speed-strength or speed-endurance.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-speed-continuum-five-zones]]; [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]; [[framework--motor-ability-connection-types-taxonomy]]; [[framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type]] (5.2, the method groups that follow from this split)\n######## heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: analytical-synthetic method and cross training\ncategory: [programming, transfer, long-term-development]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Developing Qualities Separately and Then Combining Them (and Cross Training) Suits Only Early or Transitional Stages\n\n**What it is** — The authors describe the first attempts to train the combined qualities as the \"analytical-synthetic method\": \"the qualities should be developed separately with the appropriate means and then they should be integrated into the special exercises\" (analysis breaks a process into components; synthesis combines them into a whole \"different from each of its components\"). Examples: sprints for gymnasts and weightlifters, barbell work for track athletes, and cyclic sports for general endurance (Verkhoshansky 1977). They grant these recommendations are hard to deny, but say they \"are appropriate only for the initial stages of training and it would be a serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257). Multi-faceted preparation as \"the leading training principle\" is \"partly true and only under certain circumstances,\" because it rests on observing single-factor adaptation, not on \"quantitative analysis of the interrelation among all the factors involved.\" Transfer between qualities occurs \"primarily in the initial stages\" (Yakovlev 1968; Zimkin 1965; Zatsiorsky 1965), and with rising performance multi-faceted preparation \"inevitably opposes the law of gradual development\" and \"can hinder the body's specialised adaptation\" (Verkhoshansky 1977). The authors apply this to Western \"cross training\": concurrent use of varied means or similar sports \"to prevent stagnation can be counterproductive and valuable only during particular transitional stages,\" and even then means should be combined by \"the most appropriate sequentiality, simultaneity or scheme of superposition\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257; cross training is taken up again in 5.5).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that transfer between qualities is real but limited to early training, so a programme built on transfer stops working as the athlete specializes. They give no physiological mechanism. Inference: this fits the book's broader specificity argument, where adaptation narrows toward the trained task as qualification rises.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities ... are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 182). Inference: this heuristic supports that rule for advanced athletes, and adds a time dimension: varied general means are reasonable for freshmen and for transition periods (post-season, return from a layoff), but a senior's main blocks should be organised around the sport's specific demands, with general means placed by sequence rather than mixed in to \"keep it fresh.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not read it as \"general training is useless.\" The authors keep a place for general means in early and transitional stages, and Bompa's long-term model builds multilateral development into the early years ([[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]), which agrees on that point. The book also prints a passage in Ch 2 that \"the development of each quality positively influences the development of the others\" (see [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]), so the book is not uniform on how much qualities help each other; that entry records the tension. The cited studies are 1960s Soviet work with no data given here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples: barbell exercises for track athletes and sprints for weightlifters and gymnasts are fine early, but \"a serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]; [[principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities]]; [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]] (sequencing of means); [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]]; [[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]\n######## heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: misreading dynamic correspondence\ncategory: [dynamic-correspondence, exercise-selection, transfer]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Dynamic Correspondence Means Matching the Sport's Motor Characteristics, Not Copying the Sport Movement Under Load\n\n**What it is** — The authors warn of \"an excessive passion for making the strength training means correspond structurally to the special exercises.\" The misinterpretation of dynamic correspondence \"lies in literally copying the special exercise in training movements, instead of selecting a specific arsenal of means corresponding to the most important motor characteristics of the given sport and controlling their growth by creating conditions under which they will develop steadily.\" In their words, \"it is inappropriate to try to simulate sports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abilities, but it can also alter the neuromuscular programmes which control the motor actions.\" The aim is \"the specific type of fitness and motor characteristics of the sport\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257, verified against the page image). The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up: the shape of the force-time curve depends on the neuromuscular system's ability to develop force \"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect,\" which \"requires specialised means of training,\" and the authors go as far as \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" (PDF p. 267-268 / printed p. 256-257).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that a loaded copy of a skill changes the skill's neuromuscular programme while missing the motor characteristic that matters. They give no further mechanism or data on these pages. Inference: the five criteria in 4.3 (amplitude and direction, accentuated region, dynamics of effort, rate and time of force, regime of work) are the tool for picking a \"specific arsenal of means\" by characteristic rather than by look.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). This passage is the authors' own statement of that \"not surface resemblance\" rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, a loaded sprint drill that changes the mechanics) and the question is whether it trains the quality or only resembles the skill.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not ban all resisted sport movements: in 4.3 they discuss parachute resistance for sprinters (see [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]), and in 5.2.3 they write that \"good results are achieved by making the special exercise more demanding,\" with water resistance in rowing or very small limb loads enhancing speed-strength \"under conditions that are very close to those of the special exercise\" (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267). Inference: the line is whether the load changes the movement's structure; light loads that leave the pattern intact fall outside this warning. The sweeping sentence \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" is an assertion without evidence on the page; do not quote it as a finding.\n\n**Example** — Inference: the source gives no named example here. A heavy cable \"simulated\" football block that slows and reshapes the punch would be the literal copy the authors warn against; a heavy push or pull that matches the punch's accentuated region and rate of force, plus fast unloaded punches, would be the \"arsenal of means\" approach.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation]]; [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]]; [[elite-sports-performance--evans/case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown]]\n######## framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2\"\ntopic: classifying special strength methods\ncategory: [programming, strength-qualities, classification]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Classifying Special Strength Methods: Four Sport Groups, Three Effort Methods, Five Method Groups\n\n**What it is** — The authors build the chapter's method system in three layers (PDF p. 268-269 / printed p. 257-258, verified against the page image). (1) Four categories of sport by motor regime (Diachkov 1961): sports characterised by \"the production of maximal strength\"; by \"endurance with optimal efforts of various intensities\"; by \"a high level of skill\"; and by \"the complex display of several motor abilities at different levels of development.\" This grouping \"identifies the primary emphasis of the strength training, which determines the choice of suitable methods,\" and from it come the needs for explosive strength, strength-endurance, strength-skill, relative strength and absolute strength (Diachkov 1961; Kuznetsov 1970; Chudinov 1961). It also grounds three principles: \"The magnitude of the main load,\" \"The specific need to change the training load,\" and \"The method of incorporating into a training scheme the means for exploiting the after-effect of previous work (Verkhoshansky, 1970).\" (2) Loads are split into maximal tension, speed-strength and strength-endurance loads, and maximal tension can be produced by lifting to failure or by lifting a maximal weight at maximal velocity, so \"three ways of developing strength may be distinguished, i.e. repetitive, maximal and dynamic effort (Zatsiorsky, 1966).\" (3) Methods are grouped by the strength they develop: \"absolute strength, speed-strength, explosive strength, reactive ability and strength-endurance,\" and within each group by \"the particular type of muscular contraction in the given sports exercises (see 3.1).\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale is that method choice should follow the motor regime of the sport, so classifying sports first makes the method choice systematic. They give no data for the scheme itself. Inference: the five method groups map onto the rest of Chapter 5 (5.2.1 maximum strength, 5.2.3 speed-strength, 5.2.4 explosive strength and reactive ability, 5.2.5 strength-endurance, per the section headings at PDF p. 269, 275, 278 and 296), which is how the book uses it.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): the head coach's system of play is \"treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 188). Inference: the four sport groups give a more formal first question for the same decision: which group does this sport, or this position, sit in? Football is the fourth group (several abilities at different levels), and positions differ inside it, so a lineman and a receiver may draw from different method groups.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The four-group classification is a 1961 scheme that the authors adopt without data. Inference: many team sports fall into group 4 by default, so the grouping says little there; the position-level demand matters more. The three effort methods are cited to Zatsiorsky 1966 only by name on this page; the book does not define \"dynamic effort\" here, so (Inference) do not read a later, narrower meaning of \"dynamic effort\" into it from this passage.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example list of strength types derived from the grouping: explosive strength, strength-endurance and strength-skill (Diachkov 1961; Kuznetsov 1970), and relative strength, absolute strength and strength-endurance (Chudinov 1961) (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]] (5.2.1); [[framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types]] (5.2.3); [[framework--eight-types-of-muscular-tension]] (3.1, the contraction types used inside each group); [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]] (the after-effect principle)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c283a837-a4a5-45c7-a00d-6815de7c685d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:03:13.710Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"######## claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: impulse and momentum\ncategory: [biomechanics, force-time-curve, testing]\nconfidence: proven\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Newton's Second Law and the Impulse-Momentum Relation as the Mathematical Base of the Force-Time Curve, With the Book's Loose Wording Marked\n\n**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 5 by saying that if \"the Force-Time curve is to be regarded as the graphical starting point for sport biomechanics, then Newton's 2nd Law of Motion must logically serve as the corresponding mathematical starting point\" (PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They state Newton II as \"Force is proportional to the rate of change of momentum,\" write it as equation (5.1), F = d(m.v)/dt = m.dv/dt + v.dm/dt, and reduce it to F = m.a \"if the mass of the system is not changing with time,\" which they say holds in sport (PDF p. 266-267 / printed p. 255-256; equations verified against the page image). They then separate force \"generated ballistically or impulsively\" from force \"produced more slowly\": for slow force \"it is useful to refer to the momentum m.v of the body,\" but for \"brief transient force development, it is conventional to talk about the impulse I.\" The impulse is written I = ∫F.dt = ∫m.dv, or I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV, and \"Even if neither F nor ΔT is known, the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256, verified against the page image). Scope of `proven`: only the identity that the impulse of the net force on a body of constant mass equals its change in momentum (∫F_net dt = mΔv). The sentences listed in When NOT are outside that scope.\n\n**Why it works** — Inference: this is textbook mechanics. Integrating F_net = m.dv/dt over time gives ∫F_net dt = mΔv, so the area under a net-force-time curve equals the change in momentum whatever the curve's shape. That is why the take-off velocity of a jump fixes the net impulse even when the force record is not known, which is the authors' \"even if neither F nor ΔT is known\" point. The authors give no further mechanism; they use the relation to frame the RFD comparison of two athletes repeated from Ch 2.2.2 (Fig 5.1).\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"Force plates (CMJ testing) were already the department's longitudinal performance-tracking tool\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 73), and force-plate metrics are chosen by the ODSF framework, with \"1-3 driver metrics (what produces the output, e.g. braking/propulsive force)\" (line 79, adopted vendor framework). Inference: the impulse-momentum identity is what links a driver metric such as propulsive impulse to the output metric (take-off velocity, hence jump height), so it is the right lens when a jump changes and staff ask whether the athlete produced more impulse or the same impulse in a different way. Use the authors' split as a reading rule: for brief ballistic actions (take-off, throw release, strike) think in impulse over the short contact time; for slow efforts, momentum and peak force are adequate.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Four sentences in the passage are loose and should not be quoted as physics. (a) Inference: the v.dm/dt term in equation (5.1) is not a valid general form for a system that loses mass (the correct form uses the velocity of the ejected mass relative to the body), though the book's own conclusion F = m.a for constant mass, the sport case, is right. (b) Inference: \"I = F.ΔT\" is true only if F is read as the average force over ΔT; the book does not say so. (c) The authors write that the area under athlete B's curve in Fig 5.1 is \"(i.e. the momentum)\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: the area under a force-time curve is that force's impulse; it equals a body's change in momentum only when it is the net force on a free body. In Fig 5.1 the athletes perform a maximal effort and \"hold the load until fatigue forced them to stop,\" so no body is gaining that momentum; read the area as total impulse of effort, not momentum. (d) The authors write that \"any change in direction, and not only magnitude, will also be associated with a change in force\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). Inference: any change in the velocity vector, direction alone included, requires a net force; whether the force itself changes depends on the motion (constant-speed circular motion has a constant-size force that only turns). Do not use (c) or (d) as stated.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is Fig 5.1 (verified against the page image): athlete A exerts more force than B at any instant before time T2, so A has the advantage where \"rapid RFD\" is needed (jumping, striking, throwing), while B reaches the higher peak and sustains force longer, an advantage in \"wrestling, powerlifting and scrummaging in rugby\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). This comparison is already atomized from Ch 2.2.2 in [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]].\n\n**Related** — [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]] (Fig 5.1 repeats the Ch 2 two-athlete comparison); [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]] (4.3.4, the authors' caution on comparing impulse across movements); [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]] (4.3.4, the companion warning on power); [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]]\n######## principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: independence of combined motor qualities\ncategory: [motor-abilities, speed-strength, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Speed-Strength, Strength-Endurance and Speed-Endurance Are Independent Qualities, Not By-Products of Strength, Speed and Endurance\n\n**What it is** — The authors state that \"speed-strength, strength-endurance and speed-endurance are not simply derivatives of strength, speed and endurance, but are totally independent qualities.\" Their evidence is that \"an increase in absolute strength does not necessarily enhance any of these three qualities.\" So the combined qualities \"should be placed alongside the latter in a group, each with its own particular means and methods of development (Verkhoshansky, 1963)\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257). The same paragraph frames force and velocity as qualities that, \"at a high level of development are inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4),\" and says excessive development of both \"is not required\" because they are \"interrelated aspects\" of all motor activity; depending on the movement, \"one of these qualities achieves greater development\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism beyond the observation that absolute strength gains do not carry over automatically. Inference: \"inversely proportional\" here is the authors' loose wording about trained motor qualities, not a statement of Hill's force-velocity equation, and should not be read as a law of mechanics.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Vermeil hierarchy (work capacity, strength, power, speed, sport skill, \"each level a required foundation for the next\") is the department's programming \"compass\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101, Explicit, Q1). Inference: this principle refines that compass rather than contradicting it: strength can be a foundation for power and speed, but the authors say a strength gain does not by itself buy speed-strength or strength-endurance, so each combined quality needs its own block of means. Use it when a strong athlete's jump, sprint or repeat-effort numbers stall while the squat keeps rising.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The claim is \"does not necessarily enhance,\" not \"never enhances.\" Elsewhere the book records that qualities transfer more in the early stages of training ([[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]), so for novices a general strength gain may still lift the combined qualities. The authors cite one source (Verkhoshansky 1963) and give no data on this page. Inference: do not use the \"inversely proportional\" sentence to argue that strength training slows athletes; the book's own Ch 2-4 material on the strength-speed continuum is the place for that question.\n\n**Example** — Inference: no example is given on this page. A lineman whose back squat rises over an off-season block while his 10-yard split and repeat-sprint times do not change fits the authors' claim that absolute strength gains need not carry into speed-strength or speed-endurance.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-speed-continuum-five-zones]]; [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]; [[framework--motor-ability-connection-types-taxonomy]]; [[framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type]] (5.2, the method groups that follow from this split)\n######## heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: analytical-synthetic method and cross training\ncategory: [programming, transfer, long-term-development]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Developing Qualities Separately and Then Combining Them (and Cross Training) Suits Only Early or Transitional Stages\n\n**What it is** — The authors describe the first attempts to train the combined qualities as the \"analytical-synthetic method\": \"the qualities should be developed separately with the appropriate means and then they should be integrated into the special exercises\" (analysis breaks a process into components; synthesis combines them into a whole \"different from each of its components\"). Examples: sprints for gymnasts and weightlifters, barbell work for track athletes, and cyclic sports for general endurance (Verkhoshansky 1977). They grant these recommendations are hard to deny, but say they \"are appropriate only for the initial stages of training and it would be a serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257). Multi-faceted preparation as \"the leading training principle\" is \"partly true and only under certain circumstances,\" because it rests on observing single-factor adaptation, not on \"quantitative analysis of the interrelation among all the factors involved.\" Transfer between qualities occurs \"primarily in the initial stages\" (Yakovlev 1968; Zimkin 1965; Zatsiorsky 1965), and with rising performance multi-faceted preparation \"inevitably opposes the law of gradual development\" and \"can hinder the body's specialised adaptation\" (Verkhoshansky 1977). The authors apply this to Western \"cross training\": concurrent use of varied means or similar sports \"to prevent stagnation can be counterproductive and valuable only during particular transitional stages,\" and even then means should be combined by \"the most appropriate sequentiality, simultaneity or scheme of superposition\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257; cross training is taken up again in 5.5).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that transfer between qualities is real but limited to early training, so a programme built on transfer stops working as the athlete specializes. They give no physiological mechanism. Inference: this fits the book's broader specificity argument, where adaptation narrows toward the trained task as qualification rises.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities ... are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 182). Inference: this heuristic supports that rule for advanced athletes, and adds a time dimension: varied general means are reasonable for freshmen and for transition periods (post-season, return from a layoff), but a senior's main blocks should be organised around the sport's specific demands, with general means placed by sequence rather than mixed in to \"keep it fresh.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not read it as \"general training is useless.\" The authors keep a place for general means in early and transitional stages, and Bompa's long-term model builds multilateral development into the early years ([[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]), which agrees on that point. The book also prints a passage in Ch 2 that \"the development of each quality positively influences the development of the others\" (see [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]), so the book is not uniform on how much qualities help each other; that entry records the tension. The cited studies are 1960s Soviet work with no data given here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples: barbell exercises for track athletes and sprints for weightlifters and gymnasts are fine early, but \"a serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]; [[principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities]]; [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]] (sequencing of means); [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]]; [[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]\n######## heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: misreading dynamic correspondence\ncategory: [dynamic-correspondence, exercise-selection, transfer]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Dynamic Correspondence Means Matching the Sport's Motor Characteristics, Not Copying the Sport Movement Under Load\n\n**What it is** — The authors warn of \"an excessive passion for making the strength training means correspond structurally to the special exercises.\" The misinterpretation of dynamic correspondence \"lies in literally copying the special exercise in training movements, instead of selecting a specific arsenal of means corresponding to the most important motor characteristics of the given sport and controlling their growth by creating conditions under which they will develop steadily.\" In their words, \"it is inappropriate to try to simulate sports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abilities, but it can also alter the neuromuscular programmes which control the motor actions.\" The aim is \"the specific type of fitness and motor characteristics of the sport\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257, verified against the page image). The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up: the shape of the force-time curve depends on the neuromuscular system's ability to develop force \"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect,\" which \"requires specialised means of training,\" and the authors go as far as \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" (PDF p. 267-268 / printed p. 256-257).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that a loaded copy of a skill changes the skill's neuromuscular programme while missing the motor characteristic that matters. They give no further mechanism or data on these pages. Inference: the five criteria in 4.3 (amplitude and direction, accentuated region, dynamics of effort, rate and time of force, regime of work) are the tool for picking a \"specific arsenal of means\" by characteristic rather than by look.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). This passage is the authors' own statement of that \"not surface resemblance\" rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, a loaded sprint drill that changes the mechanics) and the question is whether it trains the quality or only resembles the skill.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not ban all resisted sport movements: in 4.3 they discuss parachute resistance for sprinters (see [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]), and in 5.2.3 they write that \"good results are achieved by making the special exercise more demanding,\" with water resistance in rowing or very small limb loads enhancing speed-strength \"under conditions that are very close to those of the special exercise\" (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267). Inference: the line is whether the load changes the movement's structure; light loads that leave the pattern intact fall outside this warning. The sweeping sentence \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" is an assertion without evidence on the page; do not quote it as a finding.\n\n**Example** — Inference: the source gives no named example here. A heavy cable \"simulated\" football block that slows and reshapes the punch would be the literal copy the authors warn against; a heavy push or pull that matches the punch's accentuated region and rate of force, plus fast unloaded punches, would be the \"arsenal of means\" approach.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation]]; [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]]; [[elite-sports-performance--evans/case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown]]\n######## framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type.md\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2\"\ntopic: classifying special strength methods\ncategory: [programming, strength-qualities, classification]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Classifying Special Strength Methods: Four Sport Groups, Three Effort Methods, Five Method Groups\n\n**What it is** — The authors build the chapter's method system in three layers (PDF p. 268-269 / printed p. 257-258, verified against the page image). (1) Four categories of sport by motor regime (Diachkov 1961): sports characterised by \"the production of maximal strength\"; by \"endurance with optimal efforts of various intensities\"; by \"a high level of skill\"; and by \"the complex display of several motor abilities at different levels of development.\" This grouping \"identifies the primary emphasis of the strength training, which determines the choice of suitable methods,\" and from it come the needs for explosive strength, strength-endurance, strength-skill, relative strength and absolute strength (Diachkov 1961; Kuznetsov 1970; Chudinov 1961). It also grounds three principles: \"The magnitude of the main load,\" \"The specific need to change the training load,\" and \"The method of incorporating into a training scheme the means for exploiting the after-effect of previous work (Verkhoshansky, 1970).\" (2) Loads are split into maximal tension, speed-strength and strength-endurance loads, and maximal tension can be produced by lifting to failure or by lifting a maximal weight at maximal velocity, so \"three ways of developing strength may be distinguished, i.e. repetitive, maximal and dynamic effort (Zatsiorsky, 1966).\" (3) Methods are grouped by the strength they develop: \"absolute strength, speed-strength, explosive strength, reactive ability and strength-endurance,\" and within each group by \"the particular type of muscular contraction in the given sports exercises (see 3.1).\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale is that method choice should follow the motor regime of the sport, so classifying sports first makes the method choice systematic. They give no data for the scheme itself. Inference: the five method groups map onto the rest of Chapter 5 (5.2.1 maximum strength, 5.2.3 speed-strength, 5.2.4 explosive strength and reactive ability, 5.2.5 strength-endurance, per the section headings at PDF p. 269, 275, 278 and 296), which is how the book uses it.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): the head coach's system of play is \"treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 188). Inference: the four sport groups give a more formal first question for the same decision: which group does this sport, or this position, sit in? Football is the fourth group (several abilities at different levels), and positions differ inside it, so a lineman and a receiver may draw from different method groups.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The four-group classification is a 1961 scheme that the authors adopt without data. Inference: many team sports fall into group 4 by default, so the grouping says little there; the position-level demand matters more. The three effort methods are cited to Zatsiorsky 1966 only by name on this page; the book does not define \"dynamic effort\" here, so (Inference) do not read a later, narrower meaning of \"dynamic effort\" into it from this passage.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example list of strength types derived from the grouping: explosive strength, strength-endurance and strength-skill (Diachkov 1961; Kuznetsov 1970), and relative strength, absolute strength and strength-endurance (Chudinov 1961) (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]] (5.2.1); [[framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types]] (5.2.3); [[framework--eight-types-of-muscular-tension]] (3.1, the contraction types used inside each group); [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]] (the after-effect principle)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"05b47bfa-ca23-4ead-b41e-c6254aa4a2cc","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
