{"parentUuid":"5bc4689a-d7a8-442f-a4f3-693610c85a86","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaxGuMbuRRVdEEmqPeg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8001,"cache_read_input_tokens":105779,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8001,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":27939,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaxCJY3VfJc4QVbTCVU","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7bd02da7-ae6e-4c6e-ad9f-19fa45a2ce4d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:03:43.934Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"7bd02da7-ae6e-4c6e-ad9f-19fa45a2ce4d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaxGuMbuRRVdEEmqPeg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018qXM8NxnhbLmMi4SjdZSkx","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/ && for f in $(sed -n 6,10p ../../../_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt); do echo \"######## $f\"; sed -n '/^# /,$p' $f; grep -E '^(confidence|chapter):' $f; done","description":"Print entries 6-10"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8001,"cache_read_input_tokens":105779,"output_tokens":3418,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3212},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8001},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3418,"cache_read_input_tokens":105779,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8001,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8001,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018qXM8NxnhbLmMi4SjdZSkx":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/ && for f in $(sed -n 6,10p ../../../_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt); do echo \"######## $f\"; sed -n '/^# /,$p' $f; grep -E '^(confidence|chapter):' $f; done","description":"Print entries 6-10"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaxCJY3VfJc4QVbTCVU","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c10e1505-c24b-46bd-8380-39f64ba1feef","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:03:45.325Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c10e1505-c24b-46bd-8380-39f64ba1feef","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d50c13ce-05db-402d-a20c-8f3393d035f7","agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018qXM8NxnhbLmMi4SjdZSkx","type":"tool_result","content":"######## framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md\n# Three Methods for Maximum Strength (Repetitive Effort, Brief Maximal Tension, Isometric) and When Each Is Used\n\n**What it is** — Maximum strength in this chapter is \"the maximal force that a person can develop voluntarily,\" shown \"under isometric conditions or in a very slow movement against a maximal load.\" There are \"two basic methods\": \"the method of repetitive effort and the method of brief maximal tension\" (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), with isometrics discussed as a third option (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261). The authors' summary (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image):\n- **Repetitive effort**: lifting a weight that is raised as strength grows; \"appropriate in the initial stages of strength development and when strength and its speed of production are not important.\" Moderate weights \"(50-60% of 1RM)\" for \"8-12\" reps increase muscle mass; heavy weights \"(90-95% of 1RM)\" for \"3-6\" reps raise strength faster with a smaller mass gain. The effect grows with \"the magnitude of the load and the volume of work.\" The authors attribute its effect to proprioceptive feedback from slow lifting, strong cortex-to-muscle excitation and more motor units recruited (Kabat 1947; Hellebrant & Houtz 1956; PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258).\n- **Brief maximal tension**: \"heavy weights (85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM)\" combined with lighter weights in the session and maximal singles \"once or twice a week\"; weightlifters \"execute 5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). It raises maximum strength \"without appreciably increasing muscle mass,\" improves \"the ability to display strength quickly,\" suits athletes needing relative strength, and is used \"when the repetitive effort method has become ineffective,\" when strength must rise \"quickly in a relatively short period with a small volume of work,\" and to maintain strength and \"sporting form\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n- **Isometric tension (with gradual increase in effort)**: maximum strength without mass gain; to maintain strength \"where speed of movement is not important,\" in preparatory training, and \"during the early stages of musculoskeletal rehabilitation, when movement might be restricted.\" Isometric tension \"may be applied slowly or explosively (see 4.2.4)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is neuromuscular specificity: \"Similar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained\" by all three, \"However, the strength acquired from each of these methods has its own neuromuscular specificity\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). For brief maximal tension they say it \"develops the ability to concentrate neuromuscular effort\" and that lifting near-maximal weights \"enhances the ability to accelerate heavy loads\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). They also note that strength \"is retained for longer if it is founded first upon a base of increased muscle hypertrophy\" and declines faster if built mainly on neuromuscular efficiency (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258); that retention point is atomized in [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]].\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and %1RM prescription is kept \"stated explicitly as built on projected maxes\" (line 260, Tension 16). Inference: the percentage bands here are therefore percentages of a projected max, so the RM descriptions (3-5RM, 8-12 reps) are the safer way to apply them. The baseline's off-season rule (\"relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work,\" line 105, course material, Inferred). Inference: that rule lines up with the authors' sequence, repetitive effort first and brief maximal tension once it stops working. Inference: the in-season rule \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" (line 105) matches the authors' use of brief maximal tension \"for maintaining the existing level of strength.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The book contradicts itself on several details; keep both sides. (1) Sets: brief maximal tension needs sets \"increased to more than three (Berger, 1962)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260), but its effect \"is enhanced by increasing the maximal weight, increasing the average weight lifted per session and reducing the number of sets and repetitions\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). (2) Size of effect: brief maximal tension \"yields a larger training effect than the progressive resistance method for developing maximum strength\" (Diachkov 1961; Chudinov 1961; PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260), against \"Similar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained by the repetitive effort and brief maximal tension methods, as well as with isometric tension\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). (3) Isometrics: isometric training \"does not increase strength significantly more\" than dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260) and dynamic 3 x 5-6 \"is more effective than the corresponding amount of isometric work\" (Rasch & Morehouse 1957; Berger 1962; PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261), against the \"similar significant increases ... with isometric tension\" sentence on the same page. (4) Load-rep pairings: the 5RM is \"about 89%\" of 1RM (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), yet \"90-95% of 1RM\" is paired with \"3-6\" reps (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261), and \"85-95% of 1RM\" with \"3-5RM\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion. Inference: for sports decided by fast force production, these three methods are not the whole answer; the authors put speed-strength and explosive work in separate method groups (5.2.3-5.2.4).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own prescription for weightlifters under brief maximal tension: \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices]]; [[claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves]]; [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]; [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: contested\n######## claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md\n# DeLorme, Oxford (Zinovieff) and McCloy Progressive Resistance Schemes: Small Differences, Nothing New\n\n**What it is** — The progressive resistance method is \"a variable method of repetitive effort.\" The classical DeLorme method uses \"50% of the 10RM\" for 10 reps in set 1, \"75% of the 10RM\" in set 2 and \"the 10RM weight for the third set (Hettinger, 1961).\" The authors give the 5RM and 10RM as \"about 89% and 74% of the 1RM weights respectively (see Fig 1.9)\" (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258, verified against the page image). Variants (PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259, verified against the page image): the reverse order, \"after a warmup, moving to a maximum and then working downwards to lighter weights with more repetitions,\" is the \"Oxford or Zinovieff Method (Zinovieff, 1951)\"; the \"50% of 10RM, 10RM, 75% of 10RM sequence\" is the \"McCloy Method (McCloy, 1954).\" The authors' verdict: the modifications \"add nothing new to the DeLorme method.\" Faulkner (1950): DeLorme gave a larger strength increase than McCloy (\"154% versus 142%\"), McCloy a larger endurance increase (\"212.8% versus 186%\"). Heavy-then-light training \"elicits a larger increase in muscle size (5.5%)\" than light-then-heavy, but \"The statistical reliability of these differences, however, has not yet been corroborated, particularly with advanced athletes (McMorris & Elks, 1954).\" Krusen (1949): 25%, 50%, 75% of the 5RM for 5 reps per set \"is just as effective\" as \"the 5RM for the set 1, 125% of the 5RM in set 2 and 150% of the 5RM in set 3.\" Narikashvili et al (1960), from frog experiments and ergometry: muscles perform the same number of contractions to fatigue with a constant load as with a load built up gradually, so \"it is sensible to shorten the period of gradual increase in weight so as to reach optimal loads early and work with them throughout the training session\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for the small differences between schemes. The Narikashvili reasoning is theirs: \"the larger the load, the faster the onset of fatigue,\" so long warm-up ramps spend capacity before the working load.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): \"Prilepin's chart ... is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 107). Inference: this entry says the order of sets inside a pyramid (ascending or descending) matters little for strength, so a staff can pick the order for practical reasons (time, bar loading, technique warm-up); and the Narikashvili point argues for short ramps to the working weight.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Faulkner's percentages do not state their baseline or measure (154% of what), so read them only as a ranking, not as effect sizes. The authors themselves say the size difference is uncorroborated, especially in advanced athletes. The Krusen comparison as printed has sets at \"125%\" and \"150% of the 5RM\" for 5 reps, which no lifter can do; treat that line as unclear and do not cite it (Inference: a misprint or a different reference load would explain it, but the book gives no correction). The frog-muscle finding is a fatigue observation, not a strength-training trial. All the studies are 1945-1960 work, mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own sequence for the classical DeLorme session: 10 reps at 50% of the 10RM, 10 at 75% of the 10RM, then the 10RM (Hettinger, 1961; PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]] (5.2.2, the DeLorme-derived autoregulation scheme); [[claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices]]\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n######## claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md\n# Berger's Sets, Reps and Frequency Studies: About 3 x 6RM, One Heavy Day a Week Carries the Gain, but the Subjects Were Novices\n\n**What it is** — The authors summarise early Western studies on sets, reps and frequency (PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260, verified against the page images). Of nine bench-press programmes, \"3 sets of 6 repetitions (with the 6RM weight) was the most effective (Berger, 1962),\" corroborating Keipen (1956) on three sets of the 5RM. Berger (1963) concluded that a heavy weight \"does not permit the optimal number of repetitions,\" and light weights with many reps offer \"too weak a stimulus,\" yet \"A significant difference in strength increase was not found between training regimes using 2RM, 5RM or 10RM,\" and there was \"no major difference between the effectiveness of 2 or 3 training sessions per week.\" \"Training with a rest interval of two days between sessions is significantly more effective than with a rest interval of one day (Vasiliev, 1954).\" Berger (1965) compared five programmes: (1) 2/3 of 1RM twice a week plus the 1RM in the third workout; (2) the same with 80% of 1RM; (3) with 90%; (4) 1RM once a week; (5) 2/3 of 1RM three times a week. \"The only programme which did not produce an increase in strength was the fifth one\"; the others gave \"approximately the same\" gains. The six conclusions include: submaximal twice plus maximal once a week equals maximal three times a week; the gain from programme 1 \"is due mainly to training with the 1RM\"; with three workouts a week the load per set \"should be between the 3RM and the 10RM\"; \"Training once a week with the 1RM for 1 set increases strength significantly for up to 6 weeks\"; twice a week with 3 x 10RM equals three times a week; and after one set of the 10RM, \"subsequent lifts with lighter loads have no training value.\" The authors then stress, in italics, that \"these conclusions were based on research with untrained subjects or subjects with very little training experience\" and that \"no single programme can be ideal for everyone (Berger, 1962, 1963).\" For qualified lifters they set against this Vorobyev (1978): \"between 1-6 repetitions is the optimum number for training highly qualified weightlifters and an increase in this number hinders strength development,\" and Vorobyev (1971): most of the load is at \"70% of 1RM and higher,\" with lighter weights \"about 10% of the entire load\" (PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260).\n\n**Why it works** — Berger's explanation, as reported, is a trade-off: too heavy a load allows too few reps, too light a load is too weak a stimulus (PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259). For frequency the authors give recovery: \"the recuperative powers of the body deteriorate under the influence of a large number of workouts per week\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10). Inference: the Berger results are about novices, which is the setting where they are most usable: freshmen and athletes new to barbell training gain on modest frequency, and one heavy exposure a week can carry most of the strength gain, which helps when practice load limits lifting days. For advanced lifters the Vorobyev figures (1-6 reps, most volume at 70% or more) are the authors' own guide.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not carry the novice conclusions to trained athletes; the authors say so themselves. Several of the six conclusions are not traceable on the page: conclusion 1 speaks of \"maximal weights three times per week,\" which is none of the five listed programmes, and conclusions 3, 5 and 6 refer to data the page does not describe. Treat those as reported without their data. Berger (1962) favouring 3 x 6RM and Berger (1963) finding no difference between 2RM, 5RM and 10RM regimes are both printed on PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259 without reconciliation. The muscle tested in Berger 1965 and the study lengths are not given.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is Berger (1965) programme 4, the 1RM once a week, which raised strength about as much as the other programmes except programme 5, and conclusion 4, that one set of the 1RM once a week \"increases strength significantly for up to 6 weeks\" (PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]] (training age changes the effective load); [[heuristic--program-variable-complexity-scales-with-training-age]]\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n######## claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains.md\n# Movement Tempo Changes Strength Gains: Moderate Tempo Keeps Gains Going Longer, Mixed Tempos Gain Most, Maximal Tempo Loses Effect as Load Rises\n\n**What it is** — The authors report that \"The movement tempo of resistance exercises also has great significance for the development of muscular strength (Vorobyev, 1978).\" With a moderate tempo \"strength continues to increase over a period of 30 training sessions, whereas it increases only over a period of 15 sessions with other tempos,\" and \"the most effective variant is a combination of different movement tempos\": over 10 weeks the gain \"was 22.6 ± 0.6 kg; and 16.3 ± 0.5 kg for the execution of movements at a moderate tempo for both concentric and eccentric actions (Lelikov, 1975)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260, verified against the page image). In Vasiliev (1954), after 40 sessions of equal work in joules at 20%, 40%, 60% and 80% of 1RM \"at a tempo of 45 lifts per minute,\" results \"were virtually indistinguishable,\" with 80% slightly ahead \"but not for all of the muscle groups.\" But \"Similar training at a maximal tempo became less effective as the load was increased,\" and \"A high tempo of movement was not very effective for developing strength\"; only at 20% of 1RM, in some muscle groups, did maximal tempo match the 45-lifts-per-minute result (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). The authors frame this as: \"under certain conditions, the amount of strength developed by the repetition method is not determined by the magnitude of the load, but the quantity of work done.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for the tempo effects. Inference: a mixed-tempo programme varies the stimulus, which fits the book's wider theme that a repeated identical stimulus loses effect, but the page does not say this.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity ... specifically to avoid training monotony\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 181). Inference: the Lelikov result gives a strength reason, not only a monotony reason, to vary tempo across a block (for example, controlled-tempo and fast-intent sets of the same lift in the same week) rather than training one tempo for months.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Moderate tempo\" and \"other tempos\" are not defined in seconds or reps per minute, and the Lelikov group, exercise and test are not named, so the 22.6 versus 16.3 kg comparison cannot be scaled to another lift. The sentence order is also loose: the authors first say the \"largest increases\" come from moderate tempo, then that a combination of tempos is \"most effective\"; the numbers support the second. The equal-work part of Vasiliev (1954) overlaps the uniform-gain finding already in [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]]; only the tempo detail is new here. Inference: do not read \"high tempo was not very effective for developing strength\" as \"do not lift fast\"; it concerns maximal strength gain from the repetition method, not speed-strength, which 5.2.3 trains with light loads at maximal speed.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own figures: Lelikov (1975), 10 weeks, mixed tempos 22.6 ± 0.6 kg against 16.3 ± 0.5 kg for moderate tempo alone (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]] (5.2.3, where fast tempo is the point)\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n######## claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md\n# Ivanov (1966): Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\n\n**What it is** — In \"A 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work,\" \"the largest increase in a 1RM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric tension (9.2 kg).\" \"Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension (30.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg).\" \"Vertical jump increased only with the concentric training regime (3.7 cm)\"; it \"decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by 5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, numbers verified against the page image; the text layer misreads 30.2 as \"iJO.2\"). The authors' conclusion: \"this research does not support the effectiveness of eccentric work as much as it corroborates the principle of the neuromuscular specificity of strength, which dictates the method of its development.\" Around it they report that isometric training does not beat dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; Denison et al 1961; Asmussen 1949; Darcus 1955; Rose et al 1951), that dynamic 3 x 5-6 beats \"the corresponding amount of isometric work (Rasch & Morehouse, 1957; Berger, 1962),\" that \"Comparison with explosive isometrics, however, has not yet been done,\" and that comment on eccentric training \"should be guarded\" until \"more reliable results are obtained\" (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\": each mode trains the nervous system for that mode (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the dynamic strength index from the CMJ and the isometric mid-thigh pull \"chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 76, Explicit, Q21; tagged contested there). Inference: Ivanov's result is a reminder that an isometric test (IMTP) will rise most with isometric work and a jump test most with concentric, fast work, so a change in DSI after a block can reflect the training mode as much as a real shift in the athlete's profile. Read the two tests against what was trained.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Group sizes, subjects, exercises and loads for Ivanov (1966) are not given. Inference: a squat 1RM is decided by its concentric phase, yet eccentric training raised it most, which the authors do not discuss; that is a reason not to over-read the specificity story from one study. The \"explosive isometrics\" comparison was not done, so the isometric result applies to slow isometrics only. The book's other eccentric findings disagree in places; see [[claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own data: isometric training gave the largest isometric strength gain (30.2 kg) and lowered the vertical jump by 5.4 cm (Ivanov 1966; PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer]] (Ch 1, the same specificity point from other studies); [[claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: supported","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"5a186002-52d3-4ed2-b596-10d29d880f9a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:03:45.629Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"######## framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md\n# Three Methods for Maximum Strength (Repetitive Effort, Brief Maximal Tension, Isometric) and When Each Is Used\n\n**What it is** — Maximum strength in this chapter is \"the maximal force that a person can develop voluntarily,\" shown \"under isometric conditions or in a very slow movement against a maximal load.\" There are \"two basic methods\": \"the method of repetitive effort and the method of brief maximal tension\" (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), with isometrics discussed as a third option (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261). The authors' summary (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image):\n- **Repetitive effort**: lifting a weight that is raised as strength grows; \"appropriate in the initial stages of strength development and when strength and its speed of production are not important.\" Moderate weights \"(50-60% of 1RM)\" for \"8-12\" reps increase muscle mass; heavy weights \"(90-95% of 1RM)\" for \"3-6\" reps raise strength faster with a smaller mass gain. The effect grows with \"the magnitude of the load and the volume of work.\" The authors attribute its effect to proprioceptive feedback from slow lifting, strong cortex-to-muscle excitation and more motor units recruited (Kabat 1947; Hellebrant & Houtz 1956; PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258).\n- **Brief maximal tension**: \"heavy weights (85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM)\" combined with lighter weights in the session and maximal singles \"once or twice a week\"; weightlifters \"execute 5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). It raises maximum strength \"without appreciably increasing muscle mass,\" improves \"the ability to display strength quickly,\" suits athletes needing relative strength, and is used \"when the repetitive effort method has become ineffective,\" when strength must rise \"quickly in a relatively short period with a small volume of work,\" and to maintain strength and \"sporting form\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n- **Isometric tension (with gradual increase in effort)**: maximum strength without mass gain; to maintain strength \"where speed of movement is not important,\" in preparatory training, and \"during the early stages of musculoskeletal rehabilitation, when movement might be restricted.\" Isometric tension \"may be applied slowly or explosively (see 4.2.4)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is neuromuscular specificity: \"Similar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained\" by all three, \"However, the strength acquired from each of these methods has its own neuromuscular specificity\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). For brief maximal tension they say it \"develops the ability to concentrate neuromuscular effort\" and that lifting near-maximal weights \"enhances the ability to accelerate heavy loads\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). They also note that strength \"is retained for longer if it is founded first upon a base of increased muscle hypertrophy\" and declines faster if built mainly on neuromuscular efficiency (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258); that retention point is atomized in [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]].\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and %1RM prescription is kept \"stated explicitly as built on projected maxes\" (line 260, Tension 16). Inference: the percentage bands here are therefore percentages of a projected max, so the RM descriptions (3-5RM, 8-12 reps) are the safer way to apply them. The baseline's off-season rule (\"relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work,\" line 105, course material, Inferred). Inference: that rule lines up with the authors' sequence, repetitive effort first and brief maximal tension once it stops working. Inference: the in-season rule \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" (line 105) matches the authors' use of brief maximal tension \"for maintaining the existing level of strength.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The book contradicts itself on several details; keep both sides. (1) Sets: brief maximal tension needs sets \"increased to more than three (Berger, 1962)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260), but its effect \"is enhanced by increasing the maximal weight, increasing the average weight lifted per session and reducing the number of sets and repetitions\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). (2) Size of effect: brief maximal tension \"yields a larger training effect than the progressive resistance method for developing maximum strength\" (Diachkov 1961; Chudinov 1961; PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260), against \"Similar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained by the repetitive effort and brief maximal tension methods, as well as with isometric tension\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). (3) Isometrics: isometric training \"does not increase strength significantly more\" than dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260) and dynamic 3 x 5-6 \"is more effective than the corresponding amount of isometric work\" (Rasch & Morehouse 1957; Berger 1962; PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261), against the \"similar significant increases ... with isometric tension\" sentence on the same page. (4) Load-rep pairings: the 5RM is \"about 89%\" of 1RM (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), yet \"90-95% of 1RM\" is paired with \"3-6\" reps (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261), and \"85-95% of 1RM\" with \"3-5RM\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion. Inference: for sports decided by fast force production, these three methods are not the whole answer; the authors put speed-strength and explosive work in separate method groups (5.2.3-5.2.4).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own prescription for weightlifters under brief maximal tension: \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices]]; [[claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves]]; [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]; [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: contested\n######## claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md\n# DeLorme, Oxford (Zinovieff) and McCloy Progressive Resistance Schemes: Small Differences, Nothing New\n\n**What it is** — The progressive resistance method is \"a variable method of repetitive effort.\" The classical DeLorme method uses \"50% of the 10RM\" for 10 reps in set 1, \"75% of the 10RM\" in set 2 and \"the 10RM weight for the third set (Hettinger, 1961).\" The authors give the 5RM and 10RM as \"about 89% and 74% of the 1RM weights respectively (see Fig 1.9)\" (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258, verified against the page image). Variants (PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259, verified against the page image): the reverse order, \"after a warmup, moving to a maximum and then working downwards to lighter weights with more repetitions,\" is the \"Oxford or Zinovieff Method (Zinovieff, 1951)\"; the \"50% of 10RM, 10RM, 75% of 10RM sequence\" is the \"McCloy Method (McCloy, 1954).\" The authors' verdict: the modifications \"add nothing new to the DeLorme method.\" Faulkner (1950): DeLorme gave a larger strength increase than McCloy (\"154% versus 142%\"), McCloy a larger endurance increase (\"212.8% versus 186%\"). Heavy-then-light training \"elicits a larger increase in muscle size (5.5%)\" than light-then-heavy, but \"The statistical reliability of these differences, however, has not yet been corroborated, particularly with advanced athletes (McMorris & Elks, 1954).\" Krusen (1949): 25%, 50%, 75% of the 5RM for 5 reps per set \"is just as effective\" as \"the 5RM for the set 1, 125% of the 5RM in set 2 and 150% of the 5RM in set 3.\" Narikashvili et al (1960), from frog experiments and ergometry: muscles perform the same number of contractions to fatigue with a constant load as with a load built up gradually, so \"it is sensible to shorten the period of gradual increase in weight so as to reach optimal loads early and work with them throughout the training session\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for the small differences between schemes. The Narikashvili reasoning is theirs: \"the larger the load, the faster the onset of fatigue,\" so long warm-up ramps spend capacity before the working load.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): \"Prilepin's chart ... is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 107). Inference: this entry says the order of sets inside a pyramid (ascending or descending) matters little for strength, so a staff can pick the order for practical reasons (time, bar loading, technique warm-up); and the Narikashvili point argues for short ramps to the working weight.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Faulkner's percentages do not state their baseline or measure (154% of what), so read them only as a ranking, not as effect sizes. The authors themselves say the size difference is uncorroborated, especially in advanced athletes. The Krusen comparison as printed has sets at \"125%\" and \"150% of the 5RM\" for 5 reps, which no lifter can do; treat that line as unclear and do not cite it (Inference: a misprint or a different reference load would explain it, but the book gives no correction). The frog-muscle finding is a fatigue observation, not a strength-training trial. All the studies are 1945-1960 work, mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own sequence for the classical DeLorme session: 10 reps at 50% of the 10RM, 10 at 75% of the 10RM, then the 10RM (Hettinger, 1961; PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]] (5.2.2, the DeLorme-derived autoregulation scheme); [[claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices]]\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n######## claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md\n# Berger's Sets, Reps and Frequency Studies: About 3 x 6RM, One Heavy Day a Week Carries the Gain, but the Subjects Were Novices\n\n**What it is** — The authors summarise early Western studies on sets, reps and frequency (PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260, verified against the page images). Of nine bench-press programmes, \"3 sets of 6 repetitions (with the 6RM weight) was the most effective (Berger, 1962),\" corroborating Keipen (1956) on three sets of the 5RM. Berger (1963) concluded that a heavy weight \"does not permit the optimal number of repetitions,\" and light weights with many reps offer \"too weak a stimulus,\" yet \"A significant difference in strength increase was not found between training regimes using 2RM, 5RM or 10RM,\" and there was \"no major difference between the effectiveness of 2 or 3 training sessions per week.\" \"Training with a rest interval of two days between sessions is significantly more effective than with a rest interval of one day (Vasiliev, 1954).\" Berger (1965) compared five programmes: (1) 2/3 of 1RM twice a week plus the 1RM in the third workout; (2) the same with 80% of 1RM; (3) with 90%; (4) 1RM once a week; (5) 2/3 of 1RM three times a week. \"The only programme which did not produce an increase in strength was the fifth one\"; the others gave \"approximately the same\" gains. The six conclusions include: submaximal twice plus maximal once a week equals maximal three times a week; the gain from programme 1 \"is due mainly to training with the 1RM\"; with three workouts a week the load per set \"should be between the 3RM and the 10RM\"; \"Training once a week with the 1RM for 1 set increases strength significantly for up to 6 weeks\"; twice a week with 3 x 10RM equals three times a week; and after one set of the 10RM, \"subsequent lifts with lighter loads have no training value.\" The authors then stress, in italics, that \"these conclusions were based on research with untrained subjects or subjects with very little training experience\" and that \"no single programme can be ideal for everyone (Berger, 1962, 1963).\" For qualified lifters they set against this Vorobyev (1978): \"between 1-6 repetitions is the optimum number for training highly qualified weightlifters and an increase in this number hinders strength development,\" and Vorobyev (1971): most of the load is at \"70% of 1RM and higher,\" with lighter weights \"about 10% of the entire load\" (PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260).\n\n**Why it works** — Berger's explanation, as reported, is a trade-off: too heavy a load allows too few reps, too light a load is too weak a stimulus (PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259). For frequency the authors give recovery: \"the recuperative powers of the body deteriorate under the influence of a large number of workouts per week\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10). Inference: the Berger results are about novices, which is the setting where they are most usable: freshmen and athletes new to barbell training gain on modest frequency, and one heavy exposure a week can carry most of the strength gain, which helps when practice load limits lifting days. For advanced lifters the Vorobyev figures (1-6 reps, most volume at 70% or more) are the authors' own guide.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not carry the novice conclusions to trained athletes; the authors say so themselves. Several of the six conclusions are not traceable on the page: conclusion 1 speaks of \"maximal weights three times per week,\" which is none of the five listed programmes, and conclusions 3, 5 and 6 refer to data the page does not describe. Treat those as reported without their data. Berger (1962) favouring 3 x 6RM and Berger (1963) finding no difference between 2RM, 5RM and 10RM regimes are both printed on PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259 without reconciliation. The muscle tested in Berger 1965 and the study lengths are not given.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example is Berger (1965) programme 4, the 1RM once a week, which raised strength about as much as the other programmes except programme 5, and conclusion 4, that one set of the 1RM once a week \"increases strength significantly for up to 6 weeks\" (PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]] (training age changes the effective load); [[heuristic--program-variable-complexity-scales-with-training-age]]\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n######## claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains.md\n# Movement Tempo Changes Strength Gains: Moderate Tempo Keeps Gains Going Longer, Mixed Tempos Gain Most, Maximal Tempo Loses Effect as Load Rises\n\n**What it is** — The authors report that \"The movement tempo of resistance exercises also has great significance for the development of muscular strength (Vorobyev, 1978).\" With a moderate tempo \"strength continues to increase over a period of 30 training sessions, whereas it increases only over a period of 15 sessions with other tempos,\" and \"the most effective variant is a combination of different movement tempos\": over 10 weeks the gain \"was 22.6 ± 0.6 kg; and 16.3 ± 0.5 kg for the execution of movements at a moderate tempo for both concentric and eccentric actions (Lelikov, 1975)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260, verified against the page image). In Vasiliev (1954), after 40 sessions of equal work in joules at 20%, 40%, 60% and 80% of 1RM \"at a tempo of 45 lifts per minute,\" results \"were virtually indistinguishable,\" with 80% slightly ahead \"but not for all of the muscle groups.\" But \"Similar training at a maximal tempo became less effective as the load was increased,\" and \"A high tempo of movement was not very effective for developing strength\"; only at 20% of 1RM, in some muscle groups, did maximal tempo match the 45-lifts-per-minute result (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). The authors frame this as: \"under certain conditions, the amount of strength developed by the repetition method is not determined by the magnitude of the load, but the quantity of work done.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for the tempo effects. Inference: a mixed-tempo programme varies the stimulus, which fits the book's wider theme that a repeated identical stimulus loses effect, but the page does not say this.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity ... specifically to avoid training monotony\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 181). Inference: the Lelikov result gives a strength reason, not only a monotony reason, to vary tempo across a block (for example, controlled-tempo and fast-intent sets of the same lift in the same week) rather than training one tempo for months.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Moderate tempo\" and \"other tempos\" are not defined in seconds or reps per minute, and the Lelikov group, exercise and test are not named, so the 22.6 versus 16.3 kg comparison cannot be scaled to another lift. The sentence order is also loose: the authors first say the \"largest increases\" come from moderate tempo, then that a combination of tempos is \"most effective\"; the numbers support the second. The equal-work part of Vasiliev (1954) overlaps the uniform-gain finding already in [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]]; only the tempo detail is new here. Inference: do not read \"high tempo was not very effective for developing strength\" as \"do not lift fast\"; it concerns maximal strength gain from the repetition method, not speed-strength, which 5.2.3 trains with light loads at maximal speed.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own figures: Lelikov (1975), 10 weeks, mixed tempos 22.6 ± 0.6 kg against 16.3 ± 0.5 kg for moderate tempo alone (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]] (5.2.3, where fast tempo is the point)\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n######## claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md\n# Ivanov (1966): Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\n\n**What it is** — In \"A 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work,\" \"the largest increase in a 1RM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric tension (9.2 kg).\" \"Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension (30.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg).\" \"Vertical jump increased only with the concentric training regime (3.7 cm)\"; it \"decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by 5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, numbers verified against the page image; the text layer misreads 30.2 as \"iJO.2\"). The authors' conclusion: \"this research does not support the effectiveness of eccentric work as much as it corroborates the principle of the neuromuscular specificity of strength, which dictates the method of its development.\" Around it they report that isometric training does not beat dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; Denison et al 1961; Asmussen 1949; Darcus 1955; Rose et al 1951), that dynamic 3 x 5-6 beats \"the corresponding amount of isometric work (Rasch & Morehouse, 1957; Berger, 1962),\" that \"Comparison with explosive isometrics, however, has not yet been done,\" and that comment on eccentric training \"should be guarded\" until \"more reliable results are obtained\" (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\": each mode trains the nervous system for that mode (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the dynamic strength index from the CMJ and the isometric mid-thigh pull \"chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 76, Explicit, Q21; tagged contested there). Inference: Ivanov's result is a reminder that an isometric test (IMTP) will rise most with isometric work and a jump test most with concentric, fast work, so a change in DSI after a block can reflect the training mode as much as a real shift in the athlete's profile. Read the two tests against what was trained.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Group sizes, subjects, exercises and loads for Ivanov (1966) are not given. Inference: a squat 1RM is decided by its concentric phase, yet eccentric training raised it most, which the authors do not discuss; that is a reason not to over-read the specificity story from one study. The \"explosive isometrics\" comparison was not done, so the isometric result applies to slow isometrics only. The book's other eccentric findings disagree in places; see [[claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own data: isometric training gave the largest isometric strength gain (30.2 kg) and lowered the vertical jump by 5.4 cm (Ivanov 1966; PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer]] (Ch 1, the same specificity point from other studies); [[claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: supported","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c10e1505-c24b-46bd-8380-39f64ba1feef","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
