{"parentUuid":"d14f9cd4-0af8-4751-9c24-29eb479c88df","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffazduxrBncfYW9XnWze","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013TLcHcGHMqBRyiATS2beTe","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/ && for f in $(sed -n 11,15p ../../../_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt); do echo \"######## $f\"; sed -n '/^# /,$p' $f; grep -E '^(confidence|chapter):' $f; done","description":"Print entries 11-15"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11825,"cache_read_input_tokens":113780,"output_tokens":3015,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2809},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11825},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3015,"cache_read_input_tokens":113780,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11825,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11825,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013TLcHcGHMqBRyiATS2beTe":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/ && for f in $(sed -n 11,15p ../../../_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt); do echo \"######## $f\"; sed -n '/^# /,$p' $f; grep -E '^(confidence|chapter):' $f; done","description":"Print entries 11-15"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffazYzYUahCTKAW46fGn","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"52d9ab0f-55ad-463c-a4fb-6179493b2035","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:04:12.775Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"52d9ab0f-55ad-463c-a4fb-6179493b2035","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d50c13ce-05db-402d-a20c-8f3393d035f7","agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013TLcHcGHMqBRyiATS2beTe","type":"tool_result","content":"######## framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md\n# APRE: Autoregulating Progressive Resistance Exercise (3RM, 6RM and 10RM Routines With a Rep-Based Load Adjustment)\n\n**What it is** — APRE is the authors' expansion of Knight's (1979) DAPRE (\"Daily Adjustable Progressive Resistance Exercise\"), a DeLorme variant \"used successfully in physiotherapy\" that \"offers a combination of training and testing for the recovering patient.\" DAPRE: set 1, 10 reps at 50% of the anticipated 6RM; set 2, 6 reps at 75%; set 3, the anticipated 6RM to failure; the reps in set 3 set the load for set 4 by an adjustment table, and set 4's reps set the next workout's starting weight (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image). The authors say DAPRE \"needs to be expanded somewhat\" to fit \"the principles of supercompensation and functional fitness,\" because \"daily training of the same muscle groups using the same resistance regime is neither necessary nor recommended.\" APRE uses \"3RM (strength/power), 6RM (strength/hypertrophy) and 10RM (hypertrophy) regimes\" (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262). Four sets after a warm-up, with \"2-4 minutes of rest (preferably mild active rest with unloaded movements).\" Table 5.1 (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262, verified against the page image):\n\n| Set | 6RM routine | 10RM routine | 3RM routine |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | warm-up | warm-up | warm-up |\n| 1 | 10 reps / 50% of 6RM | 12 reps / 50% of 10RM | 6 reps / 50% of 3RM |\n| 2 | 6 reps / 75% of 6RM | 10 reps / 75% of 10RM | 3 reps / 75% of 3RM |\n| 3 | reps to failure / 6RM | reps to failure / 10RM | reps to failure / 3RM |\n| 4 | adjusted reps to failure | adjusted reps to failure | adjusted reps to failure |\n\nTable 5.2, set 4 adjustment from set 3 reps (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263, transcribed from the page image because the text layer scrambles the columns): 6RM routine, 0-2 reps decrease 2.5-5 kg, 3-4 decrease 0-2.5 kg, 5-7 leave the same, 8-12 increase 2.5-5 kg, 13 or more increase 5-7.5 kg. 10RM routine, 4-6 decrease 2.5-5 kg, 7-8 decrease 0-2.5 kg, 9-11 leave the same, 12-16 increase 2.5-5 kg, 17 or more increase 5-7.5 kg. 3RM routine, 1-2 decrease 2.5-5 kg, 3-4 leave the same, 5-6 increase 2.5-5 kg, 7 or more increase 5-10 kg. Set 4 then fixes the next session's RM load. \"Generally, the 6RM routine should be followed for 6-8 weeks, before the 3RM routine is introduced. The 10RM may be introduced at any stage to improve general muscle endurance and hypertrophy.\" As fitness rises, reps \"may be done more rapidly and a brief ballistic action may be used near the end of the movement, in order to recruit the phasic stretch reflex more powerfully\" (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale is that the athlete's own reps to failure set the load each session, so training and testing are one act, and that rotating the RM regimes avoids training the same muscles \"using the same resistance regime\" daily (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262). They give no outcome data for APRE itself; DAPRE is called \"successful\" in physiotherapy without figures.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and in return to play Applied Performance contributes \"Testing and progression\" (line 130, Explicit, Q4). Inference: APRE gives a written, rep-based alternative to projected maxes: the working RM is re-set every session from performance, with no 1RM test, which suits both the no-1RM practice and rehab progressions where Athletic Medicine wants a rule for when load goes up. The Legacy VBT protocol (line 67) did the same job with bar speed; APRE does it with reps and needs no device.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The source is inconsistent on the adjustment rule. The DAPRE text says \"If he can perform more than 6 repetitions, the weight is too light and must be increased. Conversely, if fewer than 6 repetitions can be completed strictly, the weight is too heavy,\" with adjustments \"according to a table of guidelines, as given below\" (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262), but the table below leaves the 6RM load unchanged at 5-7 reps (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263). The 10RM column has no row for 0-3 reps and the 3RM column none for 0 reps. Inference: the kg steps are absolute, so the same 2.5-5 kg change is a large step on a small-muscle lift and a small one on a squat; and to-failure sets every session add fatigue that the authors do not address for in-season athletes. Isometric and testing uses of APRE are in their own entries.\n\n**Example** — Inference, applying Table 5.2 as printed: an athlete on the 6RM routine gets 9 reps on set 3, so set 4 goes up 2.5-5 kg; if set 4 then yields 6 reps, that load stays as the next session's 6RM.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing]]; [[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]]; [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity]] (names APRE as the isokinetic alternative); [[velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am]] (daily autoregulation by bar velocity)\nchapter: \"5.2.2\"\nconfidence: contested\n######## heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing.md\n# Before APRE: Two Weeks of Skill Learning, a Bodymass-Based 6RM Pyramid Test, and RM Tests as a Cheap Stand-In for Isokinetic Testing\n\n**What it is** — The authors' protocol around APRE (PDF p. 273 and 275 / printed p. 262 and 264, verified against the page images). **Pre-testing**: \"a skills/learning phase of about two weeks is advisable\": a warm-up without weights, then \"2-3 sets comprising 8-10 repetitions with approximately 20% of bodymass for males and 10% of bodymass for females,\" heavier for large muscle groups (\"quadriceps, pectorals and erector spinae\") and lighter for small ones; in week two \"these percentages might easily be doubled\"; on the last day, test the 6RM. **Testing**: a pyramid \"beginning with 10 repetitions with approximately 40% of bodymass for males and 20% for females,\" then more weight for 8 reps, then more for 6, adding or removing weight \"until the athlete can just manage to complete the sixth repetition.\" **Execution**: concentric and eccentric phases \"at a fairly constant, slow rate (about 3-4 seconds per phase), without any sudden changes of speed or direction,\" full range, same pattern each rep. In the injured, \"limbs may be exercised separately,\" whereas in the athlete \"limbs should be exercised separately and together, so as to measure any significant imbalances.\" \"A prolonged eccentric phase (about double the normal time for lowering the load) may be used to assess the strength deficit between eccentric and isometric activity,\" which the authors call \"very important to the speed-strength athlete.\" **Muscle testing**: APRE \"can often provide an effective alternative to the extremely costly isokinetic measuring systems\" for muscle strength, \"left/right strength ratios and agonist/antagonist strength ratios,\" with \"a distinct advantage\" if done \"in sport specific or PNF ... patterns.\" \"Testing of the average person may be done quite effectively with the 6RM method, whereas the 3RM method is more useful for testing the near-maximal strength performance of serious athletes\"; the 10RM \"relates more to muscle hypertrophy and moderate local muscle endurance,\" assessed with \"girth and skinfold thickness measures.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism and no data for the bodymass percentages or the test's reliability. Their reasoning for testing is practical: an RM test in a functional pattern is cheaper than isokinetics and closer to the sport.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and \"Bodyweight-control competency is treated as a marker of injury risk in its own right\" (line 141, course material, Inferred). Inference: the two-week bodymass-scaled ramp and the 6RM pyramid give a written route to a first working load for a freshman or a returning athlete without a 1RM, and the separate-limb test gives a left/right comparison in the weight room when ForceFrame or isokinetic time is not available.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The bodymass percentages are offered as \"a rough guide,\" with no source and no split by exercise beyond large versus small muscles (Inference: for some lifts 40% of bodymass will be far from a 10-rep load). The authors give no reliability or validity data for RM-based left/right or agonist/antagonist ratios. Inference: an RM to failure is not the same quantity as an isokinetic torque, so do not compare the two numbers. The \"eccentric versus isometric strength deficit\" read from a slow lowering is named but not defined or scored. Inference: a 6RM pyramid to failure is a heavy session in itself and should not sit on a high practice-load day.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own sequence: after two weeks of skill learning, a male starts the pyramid with 10 reps at about 40% of bodymass, then 8 reps heavier, then 6 heavier, adjusting until the sixth rep is just completed (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]]; [[heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity]] (the authors' case against isokinetic testing, which already names APRE as the alternative); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]] (strength deficit, Ch 1); [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]]\nchapter: \"5.2.2\"\nconfidence: untested\n######## heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol.md\n# Isometric APRE: Hold a Load for a 6-Second Maximum (6SM), Adjust by the 6RM Table, and Train a Series of Joint Angles\n\n**What it is** — APRE \"may also be applied with an isometric training regime and may be especially valuable during the earliest stages of muscular rehabilitation, when dynamic activities may be inadvisable.\" The authors base the dose on Ivanov's data: \"the optimal combination of these variables is 6 repetitions of 100% of one's 1RM held for 6-8 seconds per repetition (Figs 5.2, 5.3)\" (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263). The protocol: hold a weight isometrically \"for a 6SM (6 second maximum) at a specified joint angle (beginning at or near full extension in most cases), with a 5-10 second rest between repetitions.\" Set 1 at 50% of the 6SM weight, set 2 at 75%, set 3 \"until the weight cannot be held for the full 6 seconds,\" and set 4 adjusted by the 6RM column of Table 5.2. \"A suitable weight is one which can be held for the full 6 seconds with every repetition.\" Because isometric training \"is highly angle specific, especially for small joint angles (see 4.2.4), it is a good idea to exercise and test the relevant joint at a series of angles between full extension and full flexion\" (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263). Figures, verified against the page images: Fig 5.2 (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263) plots isometric strength increase (kgf, about 4-14 on the axis) against hold time (4-12 s) for 40%, 60%, 80% and 100% of maximal contraction; the 100% curve is highest throughout, peaking near 6-7 s at about 13.5-14 kgf, and the 80% curve sits near 12 kgf. Fig 5.3 (PDF p. 275 / printed p. 264) plots the same gain against hold time for 1 to 6 repetitions; the 1-rep curve is lowest (levelling near 8 kgf) and the higher-rep curves peak at roughly 14-16 kgf around 6-8 s. Which of the 4-, 5- and 6-rep curves is highest cannot be read with confidence from the image.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism; the dose rests on Ivanov's curves, and the angle-series advice on the angle specificity of isometric gains discussed in 4.2.4.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the isometric seated calf raise is run with \"3 maximal 6-second contractions with 15 seconds' rest between reps\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 78, protocol, Inferred), and NordBord testing is kept for return-to-play groups \"paired with joint-specific isometric testing\" (line 134, Explicit). Inference: isometric APRE is a loading counterpart to those tests: in early rehab, when Athletic Medicine restricts movement, a 6-second hold at several joint angles, progressed by the 6RM table, gives a written progression rule that uses the same 6-second unit the department already tests with.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The text says \"100% of one's 1RM,\" while both figure captions say \"percentages of maximal contraction\"; Inference: for an isometric hold the reference is presumably maximal isometric effort, not a dynamic 1RM, but the book does not say which. The captions credit \"Ivenov\" while the text says Ivanov (Inference: probably the same author; the book does not confirm). Neither figure gives the muscle group, subjects, number of sessions or study length, so the kgf values cannot be transferred to another joint. Inference: holding a weight that would otherwise fall is a holding isometric, not the pushing-against-a-fixed-bar isometric of the department's calf and mid-thigh pull tests, so do not treat 6SM loads and those test values as the same measure. The book's earlier isometric material lists breath-holding and raised blood pressure as drawbacks (see [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]); Inference: in rehab, clear those with Athletic Medicine before using maximal holds.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own scheme: set 1 holds 50% of the 6SM weight, set 2 holds 75%, set 3 holds the 6SM weight until it can no longer be held for 6 s, and set 4 is adjusted by the 6RM rule (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]; [[claim--isometric-regional-specificity-not-strictly-angular]]; [[claim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate]]; [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--isometric-set-positions-ankle-knee-hip]]\nchapter: \"5.2.2\"\nconfidence: supported\n######## framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types.md\n# Two Groups of Speed-Strength Movements: Speed Against Small Resistance, and Effort Rapidly Developed Against Large Resistance\n\n**What it is** — The authors call speed-strength \"highly generalised and context-dependent\" and divide speed-strength movements \"roughly into two fundamental groups\": \"Movements in which speed of movement plays a fundamental role in overcoming a relatively small resistance\" and \"Movements in which effort is rapidly developed to overcome large resistance.\" \"Maximum strength is generally unimportant for the first type of movement, but it plays a decisive role in the second.\" The first group includes movements with \"a rapid reaction to external stimuli with brief contractions or with frequent repetitive contractions.\" The second is split by tension type (3.1): \"explosive-isometric tension (associated with overcoming a relatively large load and rapidly developing maximum force); explosive-ballistic tension (the rapid overcoming of low resistance) and explosive reactive-ballistic tension (where the working effect is produced immediately after a preliminary muscular stretch).\" Starting strength and reactive ability are deferred to 5.2.4. The authors add that speed-strength \"has a high degree of specificity, its transference from one movement to another being relatively poor and being developed relatively slowly,\" and its methods \"theoretically are far from being thoroughly understood\" (PDF p. 275 / printed p. 264, verified against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give the grouping as a classification, not a mechanism. Inference: the rule about maximum strength follows from the load (where resistance is small, speed limits the result; where it is large, force does), and this matches the force-velocity reasoning in the book's strength-speed continuum, but the page itself gives no data.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the dynamic strength index decides \"whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 76, Explicit, Q21; contested there). Inference: the two groups give a task-side check on that athlete-side test: if the position's key actions are group 1 (fast moves against small resistance, such as a receiver's release), maximal strength is \"generally unimportant\" for them by the authors' rule; if they are group 2 (a lineman driving a defender), maximal strength is \"decisive.\" The plyometric-versus-strength choice should then follow both the test and the task group.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The text (PDF p. 275 / printed p. 264) places explosive-ballistic tension (\"the rapid overcoming of low resistance\") inside the second, large-resistance group, which sits awkwardly with the group definitions; the book does not explain this. Inference: read the three tension types as subdivisions of speed-strength in general, not strictly of group 2. The grouping is \"rough\" by the authors' own word, and many sport actions mix both groups within one movement.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own definitions: explosive-isometric tension for \"overcoming a relatively large load and rapidly developing maximum force,\" and explosive reactive-ballistic tension \"where the working effect is produced immediately after a preliminary muscular stretch\" (PDF p. 275 / printed p. 264).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--eight-types-of-muscular-tension]] (3.1, where the tension types are defined); [[framework--strength-speed-continuum-five-zones]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]; [[framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/claim--strength-speed-correlation-depends-on-external-resistance]] (Zatsiorsky's parallel finding that strength matters more as resistance rises)\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: supported\n######## heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md\n# Speed-Strength Loading: Mostly About 20% of 1RM at Maximal Acceleration, Mixed 5:1 With Loads up to 40%, Plus Fast Isometrics at 60-80%\n\n**What it is** — \"The development of speed-strength is more effective the more speed loading there is in training and the shorter the time spent using slow movements (Yakovlev et al, 1960).\" \"Exercise with small loads (with approximately 20% of 1RM) is the principal method of developing speed-strength\" (Korobkov 1953; Vasiliev 1954; Gerasimov, Yakhontov 1954; Zimkin 1956; Agdgomelashvili 1964; Butenko 1967) (PDF p. 275-276 / printed p. 264-265, verified against the page images). With it, \"speed of movement increases with and without loads, and experiments have shown that the general increase in speed can reach 146% of the initial level.\" \"Movements should be executed with maximal effort and the apparatus should be accelerated as rapidly as possible.\" To promote recruitment, light and heavier weights \"(up to 40% of 1RM) should be combined (the variable method)\" with \"emphasis on acceleration at the beginning of the movement,\" plus plyometrics and \"exercises where isometric tension is generated quickly using 60-80% of 1RM.\" \"The optimal combination of the volume of exercises with light weights and relatively heavy weights should be in a ratio of 5:1,\" and \"the alternation of exercises is most effective for the ranked athlete\" (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265). The section summary repeats it: \"exercises, primarily with small resistance (20% of 1RM) and, for acyclic activities of brief duration, a combination of these exercises with weights up to 40% of 1RM in a ratio of 5 to 1,\" with the regime matching the special exercise (cyclic or acyclic) and its starting condition (\"muscles relaxed, in a state of preliminary tension or stretched\") (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267). The load-versus-speed conflict may be resolved if strength opposes \"not the weight (gravitational force), but its inertia,\" a method \"very seldom used in practice\" (PDF p. 277 / printed p. 266).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are training specificity (more fast loading, less slow work) and recruitment \"in the active state\" from mixing light and heavier loads. They give no physiological mechanism beyond that.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): \"already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" in the off-season (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105), and training time is \"weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (line 104). Inference: the 20% load, maximal acceleration and the 5:1 light-to-heavier ratio give a concrete dose for that speed-strength block, for example jump squats or throws with light loads making up most of the volume and loads up to 40% the rest.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"146% of the initial level\" is ambiguous (to 146% or by 146%), the speed measure is not named, and the cited studies are 1953-1967; quote it as written, not as an effect size. The \"5:1\" ratio has no cited source on the page. \"Alternation ... is most effective for the ranked athlete\" gives no data or definition of \"ranked.\" Percentages of 1RM rest on a 1RM the department does not test (line 61, Explicit, Q10), so the 20% and 40% loads are percentages of a projected max. The inertia method is atomized in [[framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia]]; the authors themselves say they cannot give \"specific recommendations for its application.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors' own summary prescription for brief acyclic actions: mainly 20% of 1RM, combined with loads up to 40% of 1RM in a ratio of 5 to 1 (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types]]; [[framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia]]; [[claim--power-output-peaks-then-declines-with-load-jump-vs-leg-press]]; [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]]; [[claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass]]; [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]]\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: supported","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b7b76cde-5193-4ea9-ad80-6aff18de1908","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:04:13.043Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"######## framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md\n# APRE: Autoregulating Progressive Resistance Exercise (3RM, 6RM and 10RM Routines With a Rep-Based Load Adjustment)\n\n**What it is** — APRE is the authors' expansion of Knight's (1979) DAPRE (\"Daily Adjustable Progressive Resistance Exercise\"), a DeLorme variant \"used successfully in physiotherapy\" that \"offers a combination of training and testing for the recovering patient.\" DAPRE: set 1, 10 reps at 50% of the anticipated 6RM; set 2, 6 reps at 75%; set 3, the anticipated 6RM to failure; the reps in set 3 set the load for set 4 by an adjustment table, and set 4's reps set the next workout's starting weight (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image). The authors say DAPRE \"needs to be expanded somewhat\" to fit \"the principles of supercompensation and functional fitness,\" because \"daily training of the same muscle groups using the same resistance regime is neither necessary nor recommended.\" APRE uses \"3RM (strength/power), 6RM (strength/hypertrophy) and 10RM (hypertrophy) regimes\" (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262). Four sets after a warm-up, with \"2-4 minutes of rest (preferably mild active rest with unloaded movements).\" Table 5.1 (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262, verified against the page image):\n\n| Set | 6RM routine | 10RM routine | 3RM routine |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | warm-up | warm-up | warm-up |\n| 1 | 10 reps / 50% of 6RM | 12 reps / 50% of 10RM | 6 reps / 50% of 3RM |\n| 2 | 6 reps / 75% of 6RM | 10 reps / 75% of 10RM | 3 reps / 75% of 3RM |\n| 3 | reps to failure / 6RM | reps to failure / 10RM | reps to failure / 3RM |\n| 4 | adjusted reps to failure | adjusted reps to failure | adjusted reps to failure |\n\nTable 5.2, set 4 adjustment from set 3 reps (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263, transcribed from the page image because the text layer scrambles the columns): 6RM routine, 0-2 reps decrease 2.5-5 kg, 3-4 decrease 0-2.5 kg, 5-7 leave the same, 8-12 increase 2.5-5 kg, 13 or more increase 5-7.5 kg. 10RM routine, 4-6 decrease 2.5-5 kg, 7-8 decrease 0-2.5 kg, 9-11 leave the same, 12-16 increase 2.5-5 kg, 17 or more increase 5-7.5 kg. 3RM routine, 1-2 decrease 2.5-5 kg, 3-4 leave the same, 5-6 increase 2.5-5 kg, 7 or more increase 5-10 kg. Set 4 then fixes the next session's RM load. \"Generally, the 6RM routine should be followed for 6-8 weeks, before the 3RM routine is introduced. The 10RM may be introduced at any stage to improve general muscle endurance and hypertrophy.\" As fitness rises, reps \"may be done more rapidly and a brief ballistic action may be used near the end of the movement, in order to recruit the phasic stretch reflex more powerfully\" (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale is that the athlete's own reps to failure set the load each session, so training and testing are one act, and that rotating the RM regimes avoids training the same muscles \"using the same resistance regime\" daily (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262). They give no outcome data for APRE itself; DAPRE is called \"successful\" in physiotherapy without figures.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and in return to play Applied Performance contributes \"Testing and progression\" (line 130, Explicit, Q4). Inference: APRE gives a written, rep-based alternative to projected maxes: the working RM is re-set every session from performance, with no 1RM test, which suits both the no-1RM practice and rehab progressions where Athletic Medicine wants a rule for when load goes up. The Legacy VBT protocol (line 67) did the same job with bar speed; APRE does it with reps and needs no device.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The source is inconsistent on the adjustment rule. The DAPRE text says \"If he can perform more than 6 repetitions, the weight is too light and must be increased. Conversely, if fewer than 6 repetitions can be completed strictly, the weight is too heavy,\" with adjustments \"according to a table of guidelines, as given below\" (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262), but the table below leaves the 6RM load unchanged at 5-7 reps (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263). The 10RM column has no row for 0-3 reps and the 3RM column none for 0 reps. Inference: the kg steps are absolute, so the same 2.5-5 kg change is a large step on a small-muscle lift and a small one on a squat; and to-failure sets every session add fatigue that the authors do not address for in-season athletes. Isometric and testing uses of APRE are in their own entries.\n\n**Example** — Inference, applying Table 5.2 as printed: an athlete on the 6RM routine gets 9 reps on set 3, so set 4 goes up 2.5-5 kg; if set 4 then yields 6 reps, that load stays as the next session's 6RM.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing]]; [[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]]; [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity]] (names APRE as the isokinetic alternative); [[velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am]] (daily autoregulation by bar velocity)\nchapter: \"5.2.2\"\nconfidence: contested\n######## heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing.md\n# Before APRE: Two Weeks of Skill Learning, a Bodymass-Based 6RM Pyramid Test, and RM Tests as a Cheap Stand-In for Isokinetic Testing\n\n**What it is** — The authors' protocol around APRE (PDF p. 273 and 275 / printed p. 262 and 264, verified against the page images). **Pre-testing**: \"a skills/learning phase of about two weeks is advisable\": a warm-up without weights, then \"2-3 sets comprising 8-10 repetitions with approximately 20% of bodymass for males and 10% of bodymass for females,\" heavier for large muscle groups (\"quadriceps, pectorals and erector spinae\") and lighter for small ones; in week two \"these percentages might easily be doubled\"; on the last day, test the 6RM. **Testing**: a pyramid \"beginning with 10 repetitions with approximately 40% of bodymass for males and 20% for females,\" then more weight for 8 reps, then more for 6, adding or removing weight \"until the athlete can just manage to complete the sixth repetition.\" **Execution**: concentric and eccentric phases \"at a fairly constant, slow rate (about 3-4 seconds per phase), without any sudden changes of speed or direction,\" full range, same pattern each rep. In the injured, \"limbs may be exercised separately,\" whereas in the athlete \"limbs should be exercised separately and together, so as to measure any significant imbalances.\" \"A prolonged eccentric phase (about double the normal time for lowering the load) may be used to assess the strength deficit between eccentric and isometric activity,\" which the authors call \"very important to the speed-strength athlete.\" **Muscle testing**: APRE \"can often provide an effective alternative to the extremely costly isokinetic measuring systems\" for muscle strength, \"left/right strength ratios and agonist/antagonist strength ratios,\" with \"a distinct advantage\" if done \"in sport specific or PNF ... patterns.\" \"Testing of the average person may be done quite effectively with the 6RM method, whereas the 3RM method is more useful for testing the near-maximal strength performance of serious athletes\"; the 10RM \"relates more to muscle hypertrophy and moderate local muscle endurance,\" assessed with \"girth and skinfold thickness measures.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism and no data for the bodymass percentages or the test's reliability. Their reasoning for testing is practical: an RM test in a functional pattern is cheaper than isokinetics and closer to the sport.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and \"Bodyweight-control competency is treated as a marker of injury risk in its own right\" (line 141, course material, Inferred). Inference: the two-week bodymass-scaled ramp and the 6RM pyramid give a written route to a first working load for a freshman or a returning athlete without a 1RM, and the separate-limb test gives a left/right comparison in the weight room when ForceFrame or isokinetic time is not available.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The bodymass percentages are offered as \"a rough guide,\" with no source and no split by exercise beyond large versus small muscles (Inference: for some lifts 40% of bodymass will be far from a 10-rep load). The authors give no reliability or validity data for RM-based left/right or agonist/antagonist ratios. Inference: an RM to failure is not the same quantity as an isokinetic torque, so do not compare the two numbers. The \"eccentric versus isometric strength deficit\" read from a slow lowering is named but not defined or scored. Inference: a 6RM pyramid to failure is a heavy session in itself and should not sit on a high practice-load day.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own sequence: after two weeks of skill learning, a male starts the pyramid with 10 reps at about 40% of bodymass, then 8 reps heavier, then 6 heavier, adjusting until the sixth rep is just completed (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]]; [[heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity]] (the authors' case against isokinetic testing, which already names APRE as the alternative); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]] (strength deficit, Ch 1); [[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]]\nchapter: \"5.2.2\"\nconfidence: untested\n######## heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol.md\n# Isometric APRE: Hold a Load for a 6-Second Maximum (6SM), Adjust by the 6RM Table, and Train a Series of Joint Angles\n\n**What it is** — APRE \"may also be applied with an isometric training regime and may be especially valuable during the earliest stages of muscular rehabilitation, when dynamic activities may be inadvisable.\" The authors base the dose on Ivanov's data: \"the optimal combination of these variables is 6 repetitions of 100% of one's 1RM held for 6-8 seconds per repetition (Figs 5.2, 5.3)\" (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263). The protocol: hold a weight isometrically \"for a 6SM (6 second maximum) at a specified joint angle (beginning at or near full extension in most cases), with a 5-10 second rest between repetitions.\" Set 1 at 50% of the 6SM weight, set 2 at 75%, set 3 \"until the weight cannot be held for the full 6 seconds,\" and set 4 adjusted by the 6RM column of Table 5.2. \"A suitable weight is one which can be held for the full 6 seconds with every repetition.\" Because isometric training \"is highly angle specific, especially for small joint angles (see 4.2.4), it is a good idea to exercise and test the relevant joint at a series of angles between full extension and full flexion\" (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263). Figures, verified against the page images: Fig 5.2 (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263) plots isometric strength increase (kgf, about 4-14 on the axis) against hold time (4-12 s) for 40%, 60%, 80% and 100% of maximal contraction; the 100% curve is highest throughout, peaking near 6-7 s at about 13.5-14 kgf, and the 80% curve sits near 12 kgf. Fig 5.3 (PDF p. 275 / printed p. 264) plots the same gain against hold time for 1 to 6 repetitions; the 1-rep curve is lowest (levelling near 8 kgf) and the higher-rep curves peak at roughly 14-16 kgf around 6-8 s. Which of the 4-, 5- and 6-rep curves is highest cannot be read with confidence from the image.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism; the dose rests on Ivanov's curves, and the angle-series advice on the angle specificity of isometric gains discussed in 4.2.4.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the isometric seated calf raise is run with \"3 maximal 6-second contractions with 15 seconds' rest between reps\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 78, protocol, Inferred), and NordBord testing is kept for return-to-play groups \"paired with joint-specific isometric testing\" (line 134, Explicit). Inference: isometric APRE is a loading counterpart to those tests: in early rehab, when Athletic Medicine restricts movement, a 6-second hold at several joint angles, progressed by the 6RM table, gives a written progression rule that uses the same 6-second unit the department already tests with.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The text says \"100% of one's 1RM,\" while both figure captions say \"percentages of maximal contraction\"; Inference: for an isometric hold the reference is presumably maximal isometric effort, not a dynamic 1RM, but the book does not say which. The captions credit \"Ivenov\" while the text says Ivanov (Inference: probably the same author; the book does not confirm). Neither figure gives the muscle group, subjects, number of sessions or study length, so the kgf values cannot be transferred to another joint. Inference: holding a weight that would otherwise fall is a holding isometric, not the pushing-against-a-fixed-bar isometric of the department's calf and mid-thigh pull tests, so do not treat 6SM loads and those test values as the same measure. The book's earlier isometric material lists breath-holding and raised blood pressure as drawbacks (see [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]); Inference: in rehab, clear those with Athletic Medicine before using maximal holds.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own scheme: set 1 holds 50% of the 6SM weight, set 2 holds 75%, set 3 holds the 6SM weight until it can no longer be held for 6 s, and set 4 is adjusted by the 6RM rule (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]; [[claim--isometric-regional-specificity-not-strictly-angular]]; [[claim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate]]; [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--isometric-set-positions-ankle-knee-hip]]\nchapter: \"5.2.2\"\nconfidence: supported\n######## framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types.md\n# Two Groups of Speed-Strength Movements: Speed Against Small Resistance, and Effort Rapidly Developed Against Large Resistance\n\n**What it is** — The authors call speed-strength \"highly generalised and context-dependent\" and divide speed-strength movements \"roughly into two fundamental groups\": \"Movements in which speed of movement plays a fundamental role in overcoming a relatively small resistance\" and \"Movements in which effort is rapidly developed to overcome large resistance.\" \"Maximum strength is generally unimportant for the first type of movement, but it plays a decisive role in the second.\" The first group includes movements with \"a rapid reaction to external stimuli with brief contractions or with frequent repetitive contractions.\" The second is split by tension type (3.1): \"explosive-isometric tension (associated with overcoming a relatively large load and rapidly developing maximum force); explosive-ballistic tension (the rapid overcoming of low resistance) and explosive reactive-ballistic tension (where the working effect is produced immediately after a preliminary muscular stretch).\" Starting strength and reactive ability are deferred to 5.2.4. The authors add that speed-strength \"has a high degree of specificity, its transference from one movement to another being relatively poor and being developed relatively slowly,\" and its methods \"theoretically are far from being thoroughly understood\" (PDF p. 275 / printed p. 264, verified against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give the grouping as a classification, not a mechanism. Inference: the rule about maximum strength follows from the load (where resistance is small, speed limits the result; where it is large, force does), and this matches the force-velocity reasoning in the book's strength-speed continuum, but the page itself gives no data.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the dynamic strength index decides \"whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 76, Explicit, Q21; contested there). Inference: the two groups give a task-side check on that athlete-side test: if the position's key actions are group 1 (fast moves against small resistance, such as a receiver's release), maximal strength is \"generally unimportant\" for them by the authors' rule; if they are group 2 (a lineman driving a defender), maximal strength is \"decisive.\" The plyometric-versus-strength choice should then follow both the test and the task group.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The text (PDF p. 275 / printed p. 264) places explosive-ballistic tension (\"the rapid overcoming of low resistance\") inside the second, large-resistance group, which sits awkwardly with the group definitions; the book does not explain this. Inference: read the three tension types as subdivisions of speed-strength in general, not strictly of group 2. The grouping is \"rough\" by the authors' own word, and many sport actions mix both groups within one movement.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own definitions: explosive-isometric tension for \"overcoming a relatively large load and rapidly developing maximum force,\" and explosive reactive-ballistic tension \"where the working effect is produced immediately after a preliminary muscular stretch\" (PDF p. 275 / printed p. 264).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--eight-types-of-muscular-tension]] (3.1, where the tension types are defined); [[framework--strength-speed-continuum-five-zones]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]; [[framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/claim--strength-speed-correlation-depends-on-external-resistance]] (Zatsiorsky's parallel finding that strength matters more as resistance rises)\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: supported\n######## heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md\n# Speed-Strength Loading: Mostly About 20% of 1RM at Maximal Acceleration, Mixed 5:1 With Loads up to 40%, Plus Fast Isometrics at 60-80%\n\n**What it is** — \"The development of speed-strength is more effective the more speed loading there is in training and the shorter the time spent using slow movements (Yakovlev et al, 1960).\" \"Exercise with small loads (with approximately 20% of 1RM) is the principal method of developing speed-strength\" (Korobkov 1953; Vasiliev 1954; Gerasimov, Yakhontov 1954; Zimkin 1956; Agdgomelashvili 1964; Butenko 1967) (PDF p. 275-276 / printed p. 264-265, verified against the page images). With it, \"speed of movement increases with and without loads, and experiments have shown that the general increase in speed can reach 146% of the initial level.\" \"Movements should be executed with maximal effort and the apparatus should be accelerated as rapidly as possible.\" To promote recruitment, light and heavier weights \"(up to 40% of 1RM) should be combined (the variable method)\" with \"emphasis on acceleration at the beginning of the movement,\" plus plyometrics and \"exercises where isometric tension is generated quickly using 60-80% of 1RM.\" \"The optimal combination of the volume of exercises with light weights and relatively heavy weights should be in a ratio of 5:1,\" and \"the alternation of exercises is most effective for the ranked athlete\" (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265). The section summary repeats it: \"exercises, primarily with small resistance (20% of 1RM) and, for acyclic activities of brief duration, a combination of these exercises with weights up to 40% of 1RM in a ratio of 5 to 1,\" with the regime matching the special exercise (cyclic or acyclic) and its starting condition (\"muscles relaxed, in a state of preliminary tension or stretched\") (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267). The load-versus-speed conflict may be resolved if strength opposes \"not the weight (gravitational force), but its inertia,\" a method \"very seldom used in practice\" (PDF p. 277 / printed p. 266).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are training specificity (more fast loading, less slow work) and recruitment \"in the active state\" from mixing light and heavier loads. They give no physiological mechanism beyond that.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): \"already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" in the off-season (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105), and training time is \"weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (line 104). Inference: the 20% load, maximal acceleration and the 5:1 light-to-heavier ratio give a concrete dose for that speed-strength block, for example jump squats or throws with light loads making up most of the volume and loads up to 40% the rest.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"146% of the initial level\" is ambiguous (to 146% or by 146%), the speed measure is not named, and the cited studies are 1953-1967; quote it as written, not as an effect size. The \"5:1\" ratio has no cited source on the page. \"Alternation ... is most effective for the ranked athlete\" gives no data or definition of \"ranked.\" Percentages of 1RM rest on a 1RM the department does not test (line 61, Explicit, Q10), so the 20% and 40% loads are percentages of a projected max. The inertia method is atomized in [[framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia]]; the authors themselves say they cannot give \"specific recommendations for its application.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors' own summary prescription for brief acyclic actions: mainly 20% of 1RM, combined with loads up to 40% of 1RM in a ratio of 5 to 1 (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types]]; [[framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia]]; [[claim--power-output-peaks-then-declines-with-load-jump-vs-leg-press]]; [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]]; [[claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass]]; [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]]\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: supported","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"52d9ab0f-55ad-463c-a4fb-6179493b2035","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
