{"parentUuid":"15c8c78b-44e9-43be-b5cf-c47466176326","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffb2hAEDLZYsqzx2hreh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11457,"cache_read_input_tokens":125605,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11457,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":23829,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffb2a5anLckQ2sgSigdD","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"fa944925-627b-4c2d-9280-544dc0b4aee7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:04:39.770Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"fa944925-627b-4c2d-9280-544dc0b4aee7","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffb2hAEDLZYsqzx2hreh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MgG5jZo6MnHCvpjDDBav5F","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/ && for f in $(sed -n 16,19p ../../../_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt); do echo \"######## $f\"; sed -n '/^# /,$p' $f; grep -E '^(confidence|chapter):' $f; done","description":"Print entries 16-19"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11457,"cache_read_input_tokens":125605,"output_tokens":2772,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2566},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11457},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2772,"cache_read_input_tokens":125605,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11457,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11457,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01MgG5jZo6MnHCvpjDDBav5F":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/ && for f in $(sed -n 16,19p ../../../_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt); do echo \"######## $f\"; sed -n '/^# /,$p' $f; grep -E '^(confidence|chapter):' $f; done","description":"Print entries 16-19"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffb2a5anLckQ2sgSigdD","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"8cb5c97b-9f61-4913-b935-050c25f67292","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:04:41.263Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8cb5c97b-9f61-4913-b935-050c25f67292","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"13b2e94d-1b2a-4672-944b-f2e4cd21284b","agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MgG5jZo6MnHCvpjDDBav5F","type":"tool_result","content":"######## claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md\n# Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Small Deviations From the Competition Mass Work Better, and Transfer Can Run One Way\n\n**What it is** — For acyclic speed-strength the authors say \"plyometric or throwing exercises should be selected on the basis of their relevance to the sports activity,\" and give a run of implement findings (PDF p. 276-277 / printed p. 265-266, verified against the page images). Water polo throw: a 2 kg medicine ball beat a 4 kg ball, throwing distance up \"13.6% and 8.9%, respectively,\" and \"throwing a 4 kg ball is detrimental to technique (Rogener, 1961).\" Baseball: a light \"57 grams\" and heavy \"184 gm\" ball both improved throwing (regulation \"about 148 gm\"), but \"throwing the light ball improves precision for throwing the heavier ball, but the reverse effect does not occur (Egstrom et al, 1960).\" Javelin: \"The optimal weight for javelin training which does not disrupt technique is 3 kg (Matveyev, 1967).\" Shot: alternating shots \"differing in mass by 250 grams\" gave distances \"close to the mean,\" but \"this closeness to the average was not achieved with a difference of 500 grams (Ivanova, 1964; Vasiliev, 1975),\" so \"the transference of throwing speeds from a lighter to a heavier mass ... apparently is possible only by alternating the throwing of shots of different mass.\" Ice hockey: hitting a normal then a heavier puck confirmed the variable method; the heavier puck's optimal mass \"was found to be between 0.6-0.8 kg,\" selected \"for each player individually, based on the athlete's ability to hit the heavier puck efficiently (Savin, 1974).\" Table tennis: some Eastern European and Chinese players train with bats up to \"2kg,\" against typical bats of \"about 100 grams,\" only \"carefully balanced,\" by \"elite athletes about once a week before the competition phase\"; the authors warn this \"should not be emulated by less experienced coaches,\" because untutored use \"can lead to overload injuries and altered neural control patterns.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that too heavy an implement disrupts technique; they give no further mechanism. Inference: the Egstrom result (light-to-heavy transfer of precision, not the reverse) is consistent with the book's Ch 1 finding that high-velocity training transfers to low velocity better than the reverse, but the authors do not draw that link here.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). Inference: for throwing and striking positions (quarterbacks, baseball and softball), these findings argue for overweight and underweight implements kept close to the competition mass, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete by whether technique holds. Bondarchuk's correlation data point the same way: \"The greatest amounts of correlational interrelationships were most frequently found in cases where the deviations between the lighter and heavier implement weights was insignificant\" ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The shot finding is muddy as printed: \"A statistically significant difference was not found in the mean results,\" and the 250 g advantage is described only as distances \"close to the mean,\" yet the authors conclude transfer is \"possible only\" by alternating masses. Treat that conclusion as stronger than the data shown. The baseball result concerns precision, not velocity. No group sizes or training durations are given for any study. The 2 kg table-tennis bat is an anecdote from coaches, with the authors' own warning against copying it.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example: water polo players gained more throwing distance with a 2 kg medicine ball (13.6%) than with a 4 kg ball (8.9%), and the 4 kg ball harmed technique (Rogener 1961; PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265).\n\n**Related** — [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning]] (agrees: close to competition weight transfers best); [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--throws-implement-weight-transfer-near-universal]]; [[claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]; [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]]\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: supported\n######## case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md\n# Arm Throw Height After Barbell Presses: The After-Effect Experiment Behind Table 5.3 and Fig 5.4\n\n**What it is** — \"The progressive development of speed-strength is the result of combining means for exploiting the after-effect phenomenon of preceding work to increase the effectiveness of subsequent work.\" In an \"exercise modelling\" experiment, \"the working effect of an explosive movement, as measured by the height to which a load was thrown (Fig 5.4), increases by an average of 38-40% after execution of barbell presses for 3 sets of 3 repetitions with 80% of the 1RM (with a rest interval of 10 minutes between the pressing and the throwing),\" with shorter movement time and greater working path, average speed, driving force and power (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265). Table 5.3, \"Arm movement characteristics for throwing a weight before and after tonic work (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 277 / printed p. 266, verified against the page image):\n\n| Characteristic | First effort | Second effort | Difference (%) as printed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Height of throw (cm) | 44.0 | 61.0 | +38.8 |\n| Movement time (s) | 0.266 | 0.250 | -6.8 |\n| Working path (cm) | 62.0 | 65.0 | +4.8 |\n| Average speed (m/s) | 2.330 | 2.600 | +11.7 |\n| Average driving force (kgf) | 5.550 | 7.430 | +33.8 |\n| Power (kg.m/s) | 1.310 | 1.930 | +47.3 |\n\nFig 5.4, \"The after-effect of prior tonic work on the height of a thrown object (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265, verified against the page image), plots height against \"number of the movement\" (0-20): the first-effort curve runs from about 42 to about 49 cm, the second-effort curve from about 50 cm to about 67-69 cm, levelling after about movement 8. The authors conclude: \"Thus, the repetition of the same stimulus evokes a more pronounced reaction by the body and greater development of strength and speed\" (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors attribute it to the after-effect of the preceding tonic work; the mechanism is the one in their 3.4.2 after-effect material, not restated here.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): training is \"weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 104). Inference: this is the book's concrete upper-body example for pairing a heavy press with a light throw in one session (for example, heavy bench sets, about 10 minutes' rest, then medicine-ball chest passes), a pairing the department could trial and measure with throw distance or velocity before adopting it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Several numbers do not check out. Inference, recomputed from the table: 44.0 to 61.0 cm is +38.6%, not +38.8; 0.266 to 0.250 s is -6.0%, not -6.8; speed (+11.6) and force (+33.9) are rounding. The working path divided by time reproduces the average speeds (0.62/0.266 = 2.33; 0.65/0.250 = 2.60). The power row is about one tenth of average force times average speed (12.9 and 19.3 kgf.m/s) but not in exact proportion, and the book does not say how power was derived or what its unit scaling is; do not compute power that way from this table. The table is credited to Verkhoshansky (1977) and the figure to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999); the figure's second-effort heights reach about 67-69 cm while the table gives 61.0, and the book does not say whether the table is a mean or a different trial. Subjects, n, the thrown load and the throwing apparatus are not given. Inference: \"repetition of the same stimulus\" does not describe a press-then-throw design; the conclusion reads as a general after-effect statement rather than one drawn from this table. The book itself records a Western finding where shot-put performance deteriorated after preliminary throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse 1950); see [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own case: after 3 x 3 barbell presses at 80% of 1RM and 10 minutes' rest, the throw height rose from 44.0 to 61.0 cm in Table 5.3 (PDF p. 276-277 / printed p. 265-266).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]] (3.4.2, the after-effect rules); [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]]; [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: untested\n######## heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md\n# Cyclic Versus Acyclic Speed-Strength: Tempo, Rest and Fatigue Are Handled Differently\n\n**What it is** — The authors separate speed-strength in acyclic and cyclic movements (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267, verified against the page images). **Rest**: the interval is \"determined by level of fitness, the level of special endurance to repeatedly produce maximal tension and the degree of strength exerted\"; \"Experiments show that, with an optimal pause of 0.5-1 minute, speed-strength output can be maintained at a high level for a fairly long period without serious deterioration,\" and the decline with \"exhausting, monotonous work can be largely eliminated by creating a favourable emotional state.\" **Cyclic speed-strength** is \"repetitive contractions separated by relaxation phases,\" so \"the movement tempo and the duration of work are of great significance.\" \"The resistance and the movement tempo are inversely proportional\": more resistance lowers tempo and \"promotes the rapid onset of fatigue,\" so the combination of resistance and tempo \"should be selected in each specific case, based on the kinesiological structure of the given exercise.\" \"Speed of movement decreases over a long period of training done at a slow tempo and increases through training at the optimal speed (Korobkov, 1953; Monogarov, 1958).\" The criterion is \"the ability to correctly execute the full movement cycle, including the appropriate phases of tension and muscular relaxation,\" and \"The movement tempo should gradually approach the tempo of the special exercise and even exceed it, while the working time should lengthen (Diachkov, 1961).\" Making the special exercise \"more demanding\" also works: \"water resistance in rowing or very small loads on the limbs of track-and-field athletes and gymnasts.\" **Fatigue**: \"Fatigue must be avoided when one is trying to develop speed-strength for acyclic movements. However, fatigue is a necessary component of training for speed-strength in cyclic movements which require speed-endurance.\" The authors close that precise implementation \"is possible only under specific training conditions so, for the present, one has to rely on empirical methods\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are the inverse resistance-tempo relation and the need to keep relaxation phases in the cycle; they give no further mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): speed is the quality with the shortest ground-contact demand and is \"harder to train\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 96), and \"Issurin's residual training effects ... drive speed programming\" (line 97, Explicit, Q16). Inference: for a sprint or a repeated football action (cyclic or near-cyclic), the authors' rules say build tempo up to and past game tempo while lengthening the work, accept fatigue only when speed-endurance is the goal, and keep pure speed-strength (acyclic starts, jumps, throws) fresh.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"inversely proportional\" is the authors' loose wording for \"more resistance, lower tempo,\" not a measured law. The 0.5-1 minute rest comes from unnamed \"experiments\" with no exercise, load or population given; Inference: for maximal sprints or jumps that rest is short, so treat it as untested and do not apply it to maximal acyclic efforts without checking output stays high. \"Favourable emotional state\" is not defined. The authors themselves say the rules rest on \"empirical methods.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples of a more demanding special exercise: water resistance in rowing, and very small loads on the limbs of track-and-field athletes and gymnasts (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]] (4.3.3, the same \"match or exceed\" rule for effort); [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]; [[claim--three-zone-force-velocity-curve-for-cyclic-locomotion]]; [[case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters]]\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: supported\n######## case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md\n# Alternate-Leg Bounding for Sprinters: Gather the Planted Leg Through Hip Flexion, 50-100 m, With 100-150 g Thigh Cuffs (Verkhoshansky's Practice)\n\n**What it is** — \"In certain cases, power can be increased without using additional loads. For example, with jumps from one leg to the other, the push-off displays values of power which are greater than in running. Therefore, these jumps are excellent for the special strength training of sprinters.\" They must be done \"by not accentuating the push-off of the body (which does not happen in sprinting), but by active gathering-in of the planted leg (i.e. via hip flexion).\" \"These jumps should be executed repetitively over distances of 50-100 meters as fast as possible. The addition of small cuffs weighing from 100-150 grams on each thigh can makes such exercises more effective in experienced athletes (based on Verkhoshansky's experience with top Russian sprinters, such as Zubov)\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267, verified against the page image). The passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person, so this account is Siff's report of Verkhoshansky's coaching practice.\n\n**Why it works** — The given reason is that bounding push-offs show more power than running push-offs, so they overload the sprint push-off without external weight. No power values, method of measurement or source are given. Inference: the cue to gather the planted leg rather than push off keeps the drill close to sprint mechanics, which matches the book's dynamic-correspondence argument, though the passage does not say so.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: Issurin's residual effects drive \"our speed programming in the off-season and ... sprinting recommendations in season\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 97, Explicit, Q16). Inference: bounding for 50-100 m is a candidate speed-strength means for skill players in the off-season speed block, with the hip-flexion \"gather\" cue as the coaching point; thigh cuffs belong only to experienced athletes, as the authors say.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — This is one coach's experience reported second-hand, with no data; the \"greater power than running\" claim has no figures. The cuffs are reserved for \"experienced athletes.\" Inference: 50-100 m of maximal bounding is a high-volume plyometric dose for heavy athletes such as linemen, and the passage gives no set or rep count. Bondarchuk's correlation data support leg-to-leg jumps for sprinters, but are a separate source with its own population. Moyer also argues for gram-level limb loads in sprinting, but the library entry on that claim finds his kinetic-energy numbers do not hold, so it does not add support for the cuffs.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own case: Verkhoshansky's top Russian sprinters, Zubov named, bounding with 100-150 g cuffs on each thigh (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Related** — [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--sprint-jump-exercises-most-transferable-assistance]] (leg-to-leg jumps for 50 m transferred to 100 m results at all levels); [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/claim--wearable-resistance-kinetic-energy-sprint]]; [[heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation]]; [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]]\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: untested","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"521ae10a-5d91-4e58-b677-d28ae0b0ebae","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:04:41.530Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"######## claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md\n# Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Small Deviations From the Competition Mass Work Better, and Transfer Can Run One Way\n\n**What it is** — For acyclic speed-strength the authors say \"plyometric or throwing exercises should be selected on the basis of their relevance to the sports activity,\" and give a run of implement findings (PDF p. 276-277 / printed p. 265-266, verified against the page images). Water polo throw: a 2 kg medicine ball beat a 4 kg ball, throwing distance up \"13.6% and 8.9%, respectively,\" and \"throwing a 4 kg ball is detrimental to technique (Rogener, 1961).\" Baseball: a light \"57 grams\" and heavy \"184 gm\" ball both improved throwing (regulation \"about 148 gm\"), but \"throwing the light ball improves precision for throwing the heavier ball, but the reverse effect does not occur (Egstrom et al, 1960).\" Javelin: \"The optimal weight for javelin training which does not disrupt technique is 3 kg (Matveyev, 1967).\" Shot: alternating shots \"differing in mass by 250 grams\" gave distances \"close to the mean,\" but \"this closeness to the average was not achieved with a difference of 500 grams (Ivanova, 1964; Vasiliev, 1975),\" so \"the transference of throwing speeds from a lighter to a heavier mass ... apparently is possible only by alternating the throwing of shots of different mass.\" Ice hockey: hitting a normal then a heavier puck confirmed the variable method; the heavier puck's optimal mass \"was found to be between 0.6-0.8 kg,\" selected \"for each player individually, based on the athlete's ability to hit the heavier puck efficiently (Savin, 1974).\" Table tennis: some Eastern European and Chinese players train with bats up to \"2kg,\" against typical bats of \"about 100 grams,\" only \"carefully balanced,\" by \"elite athletes about once a week before the competition phase\"; the authors warn this \"should not be emulated by less experienced coaches,\" because untutored use \"can lead to overload injuries and altered neural control patterns.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that too heavy an implement disrupts technique; they give no further mechanism. Inference: the Egstrom result (light-to-heavy transfer of precision, not the reverse) is consistent with the book's Ch 1 finding that high-velocity training transfers to low velocity better than the reverse, but the authors do not draw that link here.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). Inference: for throwing and striking positions (quarterbacks, baseball and softball), these findings argue for overweight and underweight implements kept close to the competition mass, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete by whether technique holds. Bondarchuk's correlation data point the same way: \"The greatest amounts of correlational interrelationships were most frequently found in cases where the deviations between the lighter and heavier implement weights was insignificant\" ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The shot finding is muddy as printed: \"A statistically significant difference was not found in the mean results,\" and the 250 g advantage is described only as distances \"close to the mean,\" yet the authors conclude transfer is \"possible only\" by alternating masses. Treat that conclusion as stronger than the data shown. The baseball result concerns precision, not velocity. No group sizes or training durations are given for any study. The 2 kg table-tennis bat is an anecdote from coaches, with the authors' own warning against copying it.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example: water polo players gained more throwing distance with a 2 kg medicine ball (13.6%) than with a 4 kg ball (8.9%), and the 4 kg ball harmed technique (Rogener 1961; PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265).\n\n**Related** — [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning]] (agrees: close to competition weight transfers best); [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--throws-implement-weight-transfer-near-universal]]; [[claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]; [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]]\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: supported\n######## case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md\n# Arm Throw Height After Barbell Presses: The After-Effect Experiment Behind Table 5.3 and Fig 5.4\n\n**What it is** — \"The progressive development of speed-strength is the result of combining means for exploiting the after-effect phenomenon of preceding work to increase the effectiveness of subsequent work.\" In an \"exercise modelling\" experiment, \"the working effect of an explosive movement, as measured by the height to which a load was thrown (Fig 5.4), increases by an average of 38-40% after execution of barbell presses for 3 sets of 3 repetitions with 80% of the 1RM (with a rest interval of 10 minutes between the pressing and the throwing),\" with shorter movement time and greater working path, average speed, driving force and power (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265). Table 5.3, \"Arm movement characteristics for throwing a weight before and after tonic work (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 277 / printed p. 266, verified against the page image):\n\n| Characteristic | First effort | Second effort | Difference (%) as printed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Height of throw (cm) | 44.0 | 61.0 | +38.8 |\n| Movement time (s) | 0.266 | 0.250 | -6.8 |\n| Working path (cm) | 62.0 | 65.0 | +4.8 |\n| Average speed (m/s) | 2.330 | 2.600 | +11.7 |\n| Average driving force (kgf) | 5.550 | 7.430 | +33.8 |\n| Power (kg.m/s) | 1.310 | 1.930 | +47.3 |\n\nFig 5.4, \"The after-effect of prior tonic work on the height of a thrown object (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265, verified against the page image), plots height against \"number of the movement\" (0-20): the first-effort curve runs from about 42 to about 49 cm, the second-effort curve from about 50 cm to about 67-69 cm, levelling after about movement 8. The authors conclude: \"Thus, the repetition of the same stimulus evokes a more pronounced reaction by the body and greater development of strength and speed\" (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors attribute it to the after-effect of the preceding tonic work; the mechanism is the one in their 3.4.2 after-effect material, not restated here.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): training is \"weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 104). Inference: this is the book's concrete upper-body example for pairing a heavy press with a light throw in one session (for example, heavy bench sets, about 10 minutes' rest, then medicine-ball chest passes), a pairing the department could trial and measure with throw distance or velocity before adopting it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Several numbers do not check out. Inference, recomputed from the table: 44.0 to 61.0 cm is +38.6%, not +38.8; 0.266 to 0.250 s is -6.0%, not -6.8; speed (+11.6) and force (+33.9) are rounding. The working path divided by time reproduces the average speeds (0.62/0.266 = 2.33; 0.65/0.250 = 2.60). The power row is about one tenth of average force times average speed (12.9 and 19.3 kgf.m/s) but not in exact proportion, and the book does not say how power was derived or what its unit scaling is; do not compute power that way from this table. The table is credited to Verkhoshansky (1977) and the figure to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999); the figure's second-effort heights reach about 67-69 cm while the table gives 61.0, and the book does not say whether the table is a mean or a different trial. Subjects, n, the thrown load and the throwing apparatus are not given. Inference: \"repetition of the same stimulus\" does not describe a press-then-throw design; the conclusion reads as a general after-effect statement rather than one drawn from this table. The book itself records a Western finding where shot-put performance deteriorated after preliminary throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse 1950); see [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own case: after 3 x 3 barbell presses at 80% of 1RM and 10 minutes' rest, the throw height rose from 44.0 to 61.0 cm in Table 5.3 (PDF p. 276-277 / printed p. 265-266).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]] (3.4.2, the after-effect rules); [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]]; [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: untested\n######## heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md\n# Cyclic Versus Acyclic Speed-Strength: Tempo, Rest and Fatigue Are Handled Differently\n\n**What it is** — The authors separate speed-strength in acyclic and cyclic movements (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267, verified against the page images). **Rest**: the interval is \"determined by level of fitness, the level of special endurance to repeatedly produce maximal tension and the degree of strength exerted\"; \"Experiments show that, with an optimal pause of 0.5-1 minute, speed-strength output can be maintained at a high level for a fairly long period without serious deterioration,\" and the decline with \"exhausting, monotonous work can be largely eliminated by creating a favourable emotional state.\" **Cyclic speed-strength** is \"repetitive contractions separated by relaxation phases,\" so \"the movement tempo and the duration of work are of great significance.\" \"The resistance and the movement tempo are inversely proportional\": more resistance lowers tempo and \"promotes the rapid onset of fatigue,\" so the combination of resistance and tempo \"should be selected in each specific case, based on the kinesiological structure of the given exercise.\" \"Speed of movement decreases over a long period of training done at a slow tempo and increases through training at the optimal speed (Korobkov, 1953; Monogarov, 1958).\" The criterion is \"the ability to correctly execute the full movement cycle, including the appropriate phases of tension and muscular relaxation,\" and \"The movement tempo should gradually approach the tempo of the special exercise and even exceed it, while the working time should lengthen (Diachkov, 1961).\" Making the special exercise \"more demanding\" also works: \"water resistance in rowing or very small loads on the limbs of track-and-field athletes and gymnasts.\" **Fatigue**: \"Fatigue must be avoided when one is trying to develop speed-strength for acyclic movements. However, fatigue is a necessary component of training for speed-strength in cyclic movements which require speed-endurance.\" The authors close that precise implementation \"is possible only under specific training conditions so, for the present, one has to rely on empirical methods\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are the inverse resistance-tempo relation and the need to keep relaxation phases in the cycle; they give no further mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): speed is the quality with the shortest ground-contact demand and is \"harder to train\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 96), and \"Issurin's residual training effects ... drive speed programming\" (line 97, Explicit, Q16). Inference: for a sprint or a repeated football action (cyclic or near-cyclic), the authors' rules say build tempo up to and past game tempo while lengthening the work, accept fatigue only when speed-endurance is the goal, and keep pure speed-strength (acyclic starts, jumps, throws) fresh.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"inversely proportional\" is the authors' loose wording