 effective in experienced athletes (based on Verkhoshansky's experience with top Russian sprinters, such as Zubov)\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267, verified against the page image). The passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person, so this account is Siff's report of Verkhoshansky's coaching practice.\n\n**Why it works** — The given reason is that bounding push-offs show more power than running push-offs, so they overload the sprint push-off without external weight. No power values, method of measurement or source are given. Inference: the cue to gather the planted leg rather than push off keeps the drill close to sprint mechanics, which matches the book's dynamic-correspondence argument, though the passage does not say so.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: Issurin's residual effects drive \"our speed programming in the off-season and ... sprinting recommendations in season\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 97, Explicit, Q16). Inference: bounding for 50-100 m is a candidate speed-strength means for skill players in the off-season speed block, with the hip-flexion \"gather\" cue as the coaching point; thigh cuffs belong only to experienced athletes, as the authors say.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — This is one coach's experience reported second-hand, with no data; the \"greater power than running\" claim has no figures. The cuffs are reserved for \"experienced athletes.\" Inference: 50-100 m of maximal bounding is a high-volume plyometric dose for heavy athletes such as linemen, and the passage gives no set or rep count. Bondarchuk's correlation data support leg-to-leg jumps for sprinters, but are a separate source with its own population. Moyer also argues for gram-level limb loads in sprinting, but the library entry on that claim finds his kinetic-energy numbers do not hold, so it does not add support for the cuffs.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own case: Verkhoshansky's top Russian sprinters, Zubov named, bounding with 100-150 g cuffs on each thigh (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Related** — [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--sprint-jump-exercises-most-transferable-assistance]] (leg-to-leg jumps for 50 m transferred to 100 m results at all levels); [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/claim--wearable-resistance-kinetic-energy-sprint]]; [[heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation]]; [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]]\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: untested","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8cb5c97b-9f61-4913-b935-050c25f67292","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
