{"parentUuid":"0439f920-e760-4356-9163-1e5032fc3df2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"13b2e94d-1b2a-4672-944b-f2e4cd21284b","agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CAAyLoVbihu2Qjt84zwW5E","type":"tool_result","content":"== claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines econdBrain/Knowledge\n    DeLorme (1950) and Khvilon (1974) finding that gains tied to hypertrophy are retained longer\n== heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks econdBrain/Knowledge\n    individuals via prolonged breath-holding and elevated blood pressure; disturb coordination;\n    Proper breathing, adequate rest/relaxation between exercises, and limiting contraction length\n    [[principle--breath-holding-and-valsalva-support-spinal-stability-in-maximal-lifting]] (Ch3,\n== claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature econdBrain/Knowledge\n    **deteriorated** after preliminary throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950).\n    throwing event's performance got worse (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950). The coach scenario is\n== claim--wearable-resistance-kinetic-energy-sprint econdBrain/Knowledge\n    **When NOT to apply** — Do not repeat the squat-vs-wearable comparison. It is wrong in three separate ways. (1) **Units:** \"kg·m/s\" is momentum, not energy (energy\n== framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence econdBrain/Knowledge\n    nd methods of strength training should therefore give \"an adequate conditioning regime for the motor system in the special exercises.\" The foundation is the principle\n    1. the amplitude and direction of movement (4.3.1);\n    2. the accentuated region of force production (4.3.2);\n== heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity econdBrain/Knowledge\n    authors also name the APRE system (based on DeLorme's original work, see 5.2.2) as an\n== heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning econdBrain/Knowledge\n    **1. Correlations run highest close to competition weight.** \"The greatest amounts of\n== claim--sprint-jump-exercises-most-transferable-assistance econdBrain/Knowledge\n    # Among Assistance Exercises, Multi-Jump Work (Ten-Fold Jump, Leg-to-Leg Bounds) Transfers to Sprinting Most Consistently, More Than Single Jumps, Throw\n    vertical jump, triple jump from place, 10-fold jump from place, leg-to-leg jumps for 50m,\n    **leg-to-leg (bounding) jumps for 50m** as \"more transferable than other specialized-\n== claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus econdBrain/Knowledge\n    Separately, Korobkov, Gerasimov & Vasiliev (via Verkhoshansky, 1977) are cited for the\n    Vasiliev via Verkhoshansky 1977; PDF pp. 33-34, printed pp. 22-23). **Inference:** do no\n== claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer econdBrain/Knowledge\n    # High-Velocity Training Transfers to Low Velocity Better Than the Reverse; Contraction Modes B\n    high-velocity strength; high-velocity isokinetic training improves high-velocity strength\n    target quality, not default to high velocity in every case. **Eric's baseline:**\n== principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed econdBrain/Knowledge\n    chapter: \"4.3.3\"\n== claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence econdBrain/Knowledge\n    chapter: \"4.3.1\"\n== principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target econdBrain/Knowledge\n    chapter: \"2.2.2\"\n    **What it is** — The authors illustrate with a thought experiment (Fig 2.14-2.15): two","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"30f1194e-d891-45eb-b09d-a81609d61582","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:05:49.283Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"== claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines econdBrain/Knowledge\n    DeLorme (1950) and Khvilon (1974) finding that gains tied to hypertrophy are retained longer\n== heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-dr