sers/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/ && for f in framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label.md claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport.md principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md; do echo \"== $f\"; python3 -c \"\nimport re,sys;t=open('$f').read();c=re.search(r'chapter: (.*)',t).group(1);h=re.search(r'^# (.*)',t,re.M).group(1);w=re.search(r'\\*\\*What it is\\*\\* — (.{0,500})',t,re.S).group(1);print(c,'|',h);print(w.replace('\\n',' '))\"; done","description":"Inspect 5b/5c entries for overlap"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffbCXS86Et3BxJtJz6KK","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"6fe97483-7b7d-41da-8178-76286a60f32e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:06:36.140Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"6fe97483-7b7d-41da-8178-76286a60f32e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"13b2e94d-1b2a-4672-944b-f2e4cd21284b","agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L5eex1yjvJkGA8B2iF5GWn","type":"tool_result","content":"== framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md\n\"5.4\" | Four Organising Principles of Special Strength Training: Converge, Accelerate, Correspond, Maintain\nSection 5.4 opens with the premise that adaptation changes character with level. \"During the initial stages of training the body reacts to any stimulus with all of its survival mechanisms and motor abilities,\" there is \"a relatively high correlation between the various motor abilities,\" and \"The transfer of fitness is very distinct here.\" \"As sporting proficiency grows, so the adaptation response becomes more selective,\" the link between abilities weakens, and \"the direction of progress is deter\n== heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label.md\n\"5.5\" | Choose Supplementary Sports by the Fitness Qualities They Build and the Phase They Serve, Not by the Sport's Label\nThe authors define Western \"cross training\" as \"using relevant secondary sports to augment the training effect produced in an athlete's primary sport,\" and tie it to the two ways of combining training from 5.3: supplementary activities can be prescribed \"concurrently with the athlete's main sport on the same day or during the same training phase or in a definite sequence where one supplementary activity gives way to another and finally to the main sport over a given period\" (PDF p. 309 / printed\n== claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md\n\"5.3.1\" | Too Much Heavy Strength Work Lowers Speed, Explosiveness and Technique; Gains Stop After About 12-18 Weeks\nThe authors grant that general heavy resistance exercises \"enable the athlete to reach the necessary level of absolute strength, to provide general physical conditioning and to train the neuromuscular system non-specifically,\" but \"it is undesirable to use them extensively during specialised training phases, since they can diminish speed and explosiveness. High volume strength loads used after a phase of specialised jump training can substantially reduce the existing level of explosive strength \n== heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport.md\n\"5.4.1\" | Specialise Children Early by Motor Regime, Not by Sport: A Two-Stage Sport-School Model\nThe authors apply the convergence principle to youth: \"particular consideration was given to the advisability of early specialisation, not in the specific type of sport, but in the specific motor regime involved\" (Verkhoshansky 1963, 1972 cited). \"orientation of the child to the sport and physical training with the sports means does not begin with specialised exercise to achieve competitive performance standards (which can easily lead to forced training). Instead, it begins with systematic manag\n== principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md\n\"5.4.1\" | Converging the Partial Effects: Over Years, Every Means Should Steer Toward the Sport's Key Motor Ability\n\"The principle of converging the partial effects of strength training means is fundamental to the long-term organisation of strength training. It is achieved by ... the gradual convergence of their cumulative training effect to those important specific characteristics which are essential to the functioning of the body in a particular sport\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Early in the sports training process (STP), \"the tasks which determine the diversity of the training means are far apart,\" lin","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"4c6cac66-64fe-453a-afac-5fba854f9388","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:06:37.595Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"== framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md\n\"5.4\" | Four Organising Principles of Special Strength Training: Converge, Accelerate, Correspond, Maintain\nSection 5.4 opens with the premise that adaptation changes character with level. \"During the initial stages of training the body reacts to any stimulus with all of its survival mechanisms and motor abilities,\" there is \"a relatively high correlation between the various motor abilities,\" and \"The transfer of fitness is very distinct here.\" \"As sporting proficiency grows, so the adaptation response becomes more selective,\" the link between abilities weakens, and \"the direction of progress is deter\n== heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label.md\n\"5.5\" | Choose Supplementary Sports by the Fitness Qualities They Build and the Phase They Serve, Not by the Sport's Label\nThe authors define Western \"cross training\" as \"using relevant secondary sports to augment the training effect produced in an athlete's primary sport,\" and tie it to the two ways of combining training from 5.3: supplementary activities can be prescribed \"concurrently with the athlete's main sport on the same day or during the same training phase or in a definite sequence where one supplementary activity gives way to another and finally to the main sport over a given period\" (PDF p. 309 / printed\n== claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md\n\"5.3.1\" | Too Much Heavy Strength Work Lowers Speed, Explosiveness and Technique; Gains Stop After About 12-18 Weeks\nThe authors grant that general heavy resistance exercises \"enable the athlete to reach the necessary level of absolute strength, to provide general physical conditioning and to train the neuromuscular system non-specifically,\" but \"it is undesirable to use them extensively during specialised training phases, since they can diminish speed and explosiveness. High volume strength loads used after a phase of specialised jump training can substantially reduce the existing level of explosive strength \n== heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport.md\n\"5.4.1\" | Specialise Children Early by Motor Regime, Not by Sport: A Two-Stage Sport-School Model\nThe authors apply the convergence principle to youth: \"particular consideration was given to the advisability of early specialisation, not in the specific type of sport, but in the specific motor regime involved\" (Verkhoshansky 1963, 1972 cited). \"orientation of the child to the sport and physical training with the sports means does not begin with specialised exercise to achieve competitive performance standards (which can easily lead to forced training). Instead, it begins with systematic manag\n== principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md\n\"5.4.1\" | Converging the Partial Effects: Over Years, Every Means Should Steer Toward the Sport's Key Motor Ability\n\"The principle of converging the partial effects of strength training means is fundamental to the long-term organisation of strength training. It is achieved by ... the gradual convergence of their cumulative training effect to those important specific characteristics which are essential to the functioning of the body in a particular sport\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Early in the sports training process (STP), \"the tasks which determine the diversity of the training means are far apart,\" lin","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6fe97483-7b7d-41da-8178-76286a60f32e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
