{"parentUuid":"93e24843-9413-42da-a925-549b96296210","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbVwKHJwpQsayZ8WmTe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019y36rh2FbtX2LURUBbgt5B","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md","content":"# Review: Supertraining Ch 5a (5.1 to 5.2.3), independent falsification pass\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent of the writer). Date: 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 266-278 (printed 255-267). Every page in range was rendered to `staging/_renders/r5a_p266.png` ... `r5a_p278.png` and opened as an image. Every equation, table, figure and number cited below was read from the image, not the text layer.\nBaseline: every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` at the cited line (lines 61, 67, 73, 76, 78, 79, 96, 97, 101, 103, 104, 105, 107, 130, 134, 141, 181, 182, 188, 260). All quotes and line numbers match. Tension 16 is correctly named at line 260.\nLinks: every `[[wikilink]]` in the 19 files resolves to a file in the vault library or the staging library. Claims the entries make about linked entries were spot-checked (Bischke & Morehouse in the PAP entry; breath-holding in the isometric drawbacks entry; Moyer wearable-resistance entry \"wrong in three separate ways\"; Bondarchuk leg-to-leg 50 m \"all levels\"; Vasiliev uniform-gain in the threshold entry; APRE named in the isokinetic entry). All hold.\nDuplicates: searched the vault library and all staging libraries (including the 5b and 5c files in the same folder) for impulse-momentum, DeLorme, APRE/DAPRE, Berger, Ivanov 1966, Lelikov, analytical-synthetic, cross training, multi-faceted, Rogener, Egstrom, Zubov, thigh cuffs, Table 5.3, Krusen, Narikashvili, 6SM, \"20% of 1RM\", \"independent qualities\", \"literally copying\". No entry in scope restates an existing entry without a new angle. The Newton entry reuses Fig 5.1 (already atomized from Ch 2.2.2) but says so and adds the impulse-momentum angle.\n\nCounts: **GO 11, FIX 8, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Per-entry verdicts\n\n| file | verdict | problem (entry vs source, with page) | exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md | FIX | `proven` is correctly scoped to the impulse-momentum identity, and (a), (b), (c) are correct physics. Three physics sentences are imprecise. (1) The entry quotes the book's \"I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV\" and \"the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" without flagging that the book defines ΔV as \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase\" with the integral running from 0 (PDF 267 / p.256, image). m times a final velocity equals the change in momentum only if the body starts from rest. (2) When to apply: \"a driver metric such as propulsive impulse to the output metric (take-off velocity, hence jump height)\". Only the net impulse (vertical GRF minus body weight) fixes take-off velocity; GRF impulse alone does not. (3) When NOT (d): \"constant-speed circular motion has a constant-size force that only turns\". A force that turns is a changing force vector, so this does not counter the book's \"associated with a change in force\"; it confirms it. (c) also says \"net force on a free body\", which is loose. | (1) Add to When NOT as (e): \"Inference: the book defines ΔV as the final velocity of the phase and integrates from 0, so m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general impulse = m(v_final - v_initial).\" (2) Replace \"propulsive impulse\" with \"net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase)\". (3) In (d), replace the circular-motion example with: \"a projectile under gravity changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\" In (c), replace \"net force on a free body\" with \"net external force on the body\". Keep `proven` with its existing scope. |\n| principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md | GO | none | checked: \"totally independent qualities\", \"does not necessarily enhance\", \"(Verkhoshansky, 1963)\", \"inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4)\" at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); Example labelled Inference; baseline line 101 quote and Explicit Q1 tag; `supported` appropriate. Suggest reciprocal link to `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4, transfer weakens with qualification). |\n| heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md | GO | none | checked: analytical-synthetic definition, gymnasts/weightlifters sprints and barbell for track (Verkhoshansky 1977), \"appropriate only for the initial stages ... serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\", \"partly true and only under certain circumstances\", Yakovlev 1968 / Zimkin 1965 / Zatsiorsky 1965, \"inevitably opposes the law of gradual development\", cross training \"(see 5.5)\", \"sequentiality, simultaneity or scheme of superposition\" all at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); baseline line 182; Ch 2 counter-quote \"positively influences the development of the others\" present in the linked entry. Suggest reciprocal link to the 5.5 entry `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label`. |\n| heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md | FIX | (1) Location wrong. Entry: \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up\". The neuromuscular-system paragraph (\"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect ... requires specialised means of training\") closes the dynamics discussion: it follows Fig 5.1 at the foot of PDF 267 / p.256, and \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" opens PDF 268 / p.257 (image). (2) Unlabelled writer judgment and application. When to apply: \"This passage is the authors' own statement of that 'not surface resemblance' rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, ...)\" has no \"Inference:\" in that paragraph. | (1) Replace \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up\" with \"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1, sets this up\". (2) Prefix \"This passage is the authors' own statement ...\" with \"Inference:\". All quotes otherwise verified at PDF 268 / p.257 and PDF 277-278 / p.266-267 (image). |\n| framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type.md | GO | none | checked: four sport categories (Diachkov 1961), strength types with Diachkov / Kuznetsov / Chudinov, three principles incl. \"(Verkhoshansky, 1970)\", maximal tension / speed-strength / strength-endurance loads, \"repetitive, maximal and dynamic effort (Zatsiorsky, 1966)\", five method groups, \"(see 3.1)\" at PDF 269 / p.258 (image); section headings 5.2.1 at PDF 269, 5.2.3 at PDF 275, 5.2.4 at PDF 278, 5.2.5 at PDF 296 (text-layer search); baseline line 188. Football-as-group-4 is inside an Inference sentence. |\n| framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | FIX | `contested` is justified and all four contradiction pairs are on the cited pages (image): sets \"more than three (Berger, 1962)\" PDF 271 / p.260 vs \"reducing the number of sets and repetitions\" PDF 272 / p.261; \"larger training effect\" PDF 271 / p.260 vs \"Similar significant increases\" PDF 272 / p.261; Salter 1955 PDF 271 / p.260 and Rasch & Morehouse PDF 272 / p.261 vs the same \"similar\" sentence; 5RM \"about 89%\" PDF 269 / p.258, \"90-95% of 1RM ... (3-6)\" PDF 272 / p.261, \"85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM\" PDF 271 / p.260. Two problems. (1) Item (4) overstates: \"These three are not consistent\". The clear clash is 90-95% with up to 6 reps against 5RM = 89% (6 reps must sit below 89%). \"85-95% i.e. 3-5RM\" puts the 5RM near 85% against 89%, a loose mismatch only. (2) When to apply has a sentence fragment carrying a baseline quote: \"The baseline's off-season rule (\"relative strength and technique first; ...\", line 105, course material, Inferred).\" | (1) Replace \"These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion.\" with \"The 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%); the 85-95% = 3-5RM pairing is only loosely off (it implies a 5RM near 85%). Do not use any of them as a rep-to-load conversion.\" (2) Rewrite the fragment as: \"Eric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes 'relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work' (line 105, Inferred, transcript and slides).\" Also verified: Kabat 1947 / Hellebrant & Houtz 1956 at PDF 269 (image; text layer reads \"Rabat\"); Medvedev 1967 / Vorobyev 1971 \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3\" at PDF 271; baseline lines 61, 105, 260. |\n| claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | FIX | Unlabelled claim not on the page. When NOT: \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work, mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects.\" PDF 269-270 / p.258-259 gives no subject populations for Faulkner, McMorris & Elks or Krusen; only McMorris & Elks is qualified (\"particularly with advanced athletes\"). | Replace with: \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work; the book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\" Otherwise verified (image): 89% / 74% and the DeLorme 50%-75%-100% of 10RM (Hettinger 1961) at PDF 269 / p.258; Oxford/Zinovieff and McCloy sequences, \"add nothing new\", 154% vs 142%, 212.8% vs 186% (Faulkner 1950), 5.5%, McMorris & Elks 1954, Krusen 1949 25/50/75% vs 5RM/125%/150% at PDF 270 / p.259; Narikashvili 1960 at PDF 271 / p.260; baseline line 107. |\n| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | FIX | When NOT misstates traceability. Entry: \"conclusion 1 speaks of 'maximal weights three times per week,' which is none of the five listed programmes, and conclusions 3, 5 and 6 refer to data the page does not describe.\" Source PDF 270 / p.259 (image), Berger 1963 paragraph: \"with maximal weights (10RM for one set three times per week)\" and \"There was also no major difference between the effectiveness of 2 or 3 training sessions per week.\" So conclusion 1's comparator and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger 1963 sentences, and conclusion 3 (3RM to 10RM) is loosely backed by the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results. Only conclusion 6 has no data on the page. Minor: the separate p.259 sentence that \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters ... especially where strength-skill is involved\" is missing from the advanced-lifter contrast. | Replace the sentence with: \"The six conclusions are drawn partly from data outside the five-programme list: conclusion 1's 'maximal weights three times per week' and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger (1963) sentences ('10RM for one set three times per week'; 'no major difference between ... 2 or 3 training sessions per week', PDF 270 / p.259), conclusion 3 loosely to the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results, and conclusion 6 to no data on the page.\" Add after the Vorobyev (1971) clause: \"and 'other studies' show most weightlifting training for advanced lifters is done with 1-3 repetitions, 'emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved' (PDF 270 / p.259).\" Otherwise verified (image): 3 x 6RM (Berger 1962), Keipen 1956, Vasiliev 1954 rest, five programmes and \"only ... the fifth one\", conclusions 1-6, italic \"these conclusions were based on research with untrained subjects\", Vorobyev 1978 \"1-6\" at PDF 270-271 / p.259-260. |\n| claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 271 / p.260): Vorobyev 1978 tempo sentence; 30 vs 15 sessions; \"22.6 ± 0.6 kg; and 16.3 ± 0.5 kg\" (Lelikov 1975); Vasiliev 1954 equal work at 20/40/60/80% and 45 lifts/min; \"maximal tempo became less effective as the load was increased\"; 20% exception. Overlap with `claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus` correctly declared (that entry holds the uniform-gain point; this one adds tempo). Baseline line 181. |\n| claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md | FIX | Title contradicts the data and the entry's own body. Title: \"Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most\". Source PDF 272 / p.261 (image): there is no eccentric test; eccentric work gave the largest squat 1RM gain (15 kg), isometric the largest isometric gain (30.2 kg), and only concentric raised the vertical jump (3.7 cm). The entry's When NOT already notes \"a squat 1RM is decided by its concentric phase, yet eccentric training raised it most\". Minor: Why it works glosses the book's phrase with \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\", which the book does not say. | Retitle: \"Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\" (rename file to match, e.g. `claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md`, and update the four inbound links in this batch). In Why it works, prefix the gloss \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\" with \"Inference:\". Numbers verified (image): 15, 9.2, 30.2 (text layer \"iJO.2\"), 14.6, 3.7, -1.6, -5.4 cm; baseline line 76 and its contested tag. |\n| framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md | GO | none | checked: DAPRE (Knight 1979) sets at PDF 272 / p.261; APRE 3RM/6RM/10RM labels, 2-4 min rest, Table 5.1 every cell at PDF 273 / p.262 (image); Table 5.2 every cell at PDF 274 / p.263 (image): 6RM 0-2 decr 2.5-5, 3-4 decr 0-2.5, 5-7 same, 8-12 incr 2.5-5, 13+ incr 5-7.5; 10RM 4-6 decr 2.5-5, 7-8 decr 0-2.5, 9-11 same, 12-16 incr 2.5-5, 17+ incr 5-7.5; 3RM 1-2 decr 2.5-5, 3-4 same, 5-6 incr 2.5-5, 7+ incr 5-10. \"6-8 weeks\" and ballistic-end quote at PDF 274. `contested` stated reason holds: \"more than 6 ... must be increased\" PDF 273 / p.262 vs 5-7 \"leave same\" PDF 274 / p.263, both on the cited pages. Example labelled Inference and consistent with the table. Baseline lines 61, 130, 67. |\n| heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing.md | GO | none | checked (image): \"skills/learning phase of about two weeks\", 20%/10% bodymass, doubled in week 2, pyramid 40%/20%, 10-8-6, 3-4 s per phase, separate and together limbs, prolonged eccentric \"about double\", at PDF 273 / p.262; isokinetic alternative, PNF, 6RM average person / 3RM serious athletes / 10RM hypertrophy, girth and skinfold at PDF 275 / p.264. `untested` fits a protocol with no data. Baseline lines 61, 141. |\n| heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 274 / p.263): \"6 repetitions of 100% of one's 1RM held for 6-8 seconds\", 6SM, 5-10 s rest, 50%/75%/to-failure sets, 6RM column, angle-series advice; Fig 5.2 axes (kgf 4-14, time 2-14 s), 100% curve peaking about 13.8 kgf near 6 s, 80% near 12, caption \"Ivenov\". Fig 5.3 (PDF 275 / p.264): 1-rep curve near 8 kgf, top curves about 14-16 kgf; the entry is right that the 4/5/6-rep curves cannot be ranked with confidence. Caption \"percentages of maximal contraction\" vs text \"1RM\" mismatch correctly flagged. Baseline lines 78, 134. |\n| framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 275 / p.264): two groups, \"generally unimportant ... decisive role in the second\", group-1 rapid-reaction movements, three tension types with definitions, deferral to 5.2.4, \"transference ... relatively poor\", \"far from being thoroughly understood\". The explosive-ballistic placement oddity is real and correctly flagged. Baseline line 76. Nit (optional): the quote \"plays a decisive role in the second.\" cuts \"type of movement\" inside the quote marks. |\n| heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md | GO | none | checked (image): Yakovlev 1960, \"approximately 20% of 1RM\", citation list at PDF 275 / p.264; 146%, \"up to 40% of 1RM\", 60-80% fast isometrics, \"5:1\", \"ranked athlete\" at PDF 276 / p.265; inertia \"very seldom used\" at PDF 277 / p.266; summary \"20% of 1RM ... up to 40% ... 5 to 1\" and starting conditions at PDF 278 / p.267. Baseline lines 104, 105, 61. Nit (optional): the When NOT reference \"(line 61, Explicit, Q10)\" could carry the \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix. |\n| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md | FIX | Title, filename and When to apply state a rule the page's own data do not support. Entry: \"Small Deviations From the Competition Mass Work Better\" and \"argue for overweight and underweight implements kept close to the competition mass\". That is Bondarchuk's thesis. The page's examples (PDF 276-277 / p.265-266, image) include a 3 kg javelin training weight (Matveyev 1967), 2 kg vs 4 kg medicine balls for the water polo throw (Rogener 1961), a 0.6-0.8 kg heavy puck (Savin 1974) and 2 kg table-tennis bats; only the shot (250 g vs 500 g difference) and baseball (57 g / 184 g vs 148 g) are close to competition mass. The page's stated rule is relevance to the sport, a mass that \"does not disrupt technique\", and per-athlete selection of the heavy implement. All individual numbers in the body are correct. | Retitle: \"Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Expect One-Way Transfer\" and rename the file to match (e.g. `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md`; update inbound links from the speed-strength loading and arm-throw entries). In When to apply replace \"kept close to the competition mass\" with \"chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\". Keep the Bondarchuk quote but introduce it as \"Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; ...\". Add to When NOT: \"Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a 'stay close to competition mass' rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it.\" (Competition masses are outside-book knowledge, hence the label.) |\n| case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md | GO | none | checked (image): \"38-40%\", 3 x 3 at 80% 1RM, 10 min rest, Fig 5.4 caption \"(Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\", first effort about 42-49 cm, second about 50 to 67-69 cm, levelling after about movement 8 at PDF 276 / p.265; Table 5.3 every cell (44.0/61.0/+38.8; 0.266/0.250/-6.8; 62.0/65.0/+4.8; 2.330/2.600/+11.7; 5.550/7.430/+33.8; 1.310/1.930/+47.3) and \"(Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" at PDF 277 / p.266. Recomputations confirmed: +38.6%, -6.0%, 0.62/0.266 = 2.33, 0.65/0.250 = 2.60, F x v = 12.9 and 19.3. Bischke & Morehouse 1950 present in linked PAP entry. `untested` acceptable for a single unreported-n experiment. |\n| heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md | GO | none | checked (image): rest-interval determinants, \"0.5-1 minute\", \"favourable emotional state\", cyclic relaxation phases, \"inversely proportional\", Korobkov 1953 / Monogarov 1958, Diachkov 1961 \"approach the tempo ... and even exceed it\" at PDF 277 / p.266; \"more demanding\" water resistance and limb loads at PDF 277-278; fatigue rule and \"empirical methods\" at PDF 278 / p.267. Baseline lines 96, 97. |\n| case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 278 / p.267): power greater than running, hip-flexion gather cue, 50-100 m, 100-150 g thigh cuffs, \"experienced athletes\", Zubov; third-person Verkhoshansky correctly attributed to Siff; `untested` right for a coach's anecdote. Bondarchuk and Moyer cross-references match the linked entries. Baseline line 97. |\n\n## Missing (important ideas in PDF 266-278 no entry captures)\n\nCoverage is near complete. Two small gaps, neither needs a new entry:\n\n1. PDF 270 / p.259: \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved.\" Distinct from Vorobyev (1978) \"1-6\". Fold into the Berger entry (see its FIX).\n2. PDF 278 / p.267: the section's organising conclusion that perfecting speed-strength means combining two levers, \"the positive after-effect of preceding work\" and \"exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\", plus \"Thus, to develop speed-strength...\" summary. The pieces are in the loading entry and the arm-throw case, but neither states the two-lever summary. Optional one-sentence addition to `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-...` What it is.\n\n## Reciprocal-link suggestions\n\n- `heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training` and `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label` (5.5).\n- `principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities` and `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4).\n- `framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric` and `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size` (5.2.4).\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n\n1. Newton II: flag ΔV as final velocity (equals change only from rest); \"net propulsive impulse\"; replace circular-motion example in (d) with constant-gravity projectile; \"net external force on the body\" in (c).\n2. Dynamic correspondence: fix \"paragraph before\" to \"paragraph that closes\" the dynamics discussion; label \"This passage is the authors' own statement ... Use it when\" as Inference.\n3. Three methods: say which load-rep pairing clashes (90-95%/3-6 clearly; 85-95%/3-5RM loosely); rewrite the line-105 fragment with an \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix.\n4. DeLorme: remove or label \"mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects\".\n5. Berger: correct the traceability sentence (conclusions 1 and 5 trace to Berger 1963 on p.259; only 6 is orphaned); add the 1-3 rep advanced-lifter sentence.\n6. Ivanov: retitle and rename (eccentric raised squat 1RM most; no \"own test\" for eccentric); label the \"trains the nervous system for that mode\" gloss.\n7. Implement mass: retitle and rename away from \"stay close to competition mass\"; fix When to apply; add the far-from-competition counterexamples (labelled Inference).\n8. After renames in 6 and 7, update inbound wikilinks in this batch.\n\nNo NO-GO. Both `contested` entries keep both sides on the cited pages. The single `proven` entry keeps its scope.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3766,"cache_read_input_tokens":205027,"output_tokens":10203,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":462},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3766},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":10203,"cache_read_input_tokens":205027,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3766,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3766,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_019y36rh2FbtX2LURUBbgt5B":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md","content":"# Review: Supertraining Ch 5a (5.1 to 5.2.3), independent falsification pass\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent of the writer). Date: 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 266-278 (printed 255-267). Every page in range was rendered to `staging/_renders/r5a_p266.png` ... `r5a_p278.png` and opened as an image. Every equation, table, figure and number cited below was read from the image, not the text layer.\nBaseline: every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` at the cited line (lines 61, 67, 73, 76, 78, 79, 96, 97, 101, 103, 104, 105, 107, 130, 134, 141, 181, 182, 188, 260). All quotes and line numbers match. Tension 16 is correctly named at line 260.\nLinks: every `[[wikilink]]` in the 19 files resolves to a file in the vault library or the staging library. Claims the entries make about linked entries were spot-checked (Bischke & Morehouse in the PAP entry; breath-holding in the isometric drawbacks entry; Moyer wearable-resistance entry \"wrong in three separate ways\"; Bondarchuk leg-to-leg 50 m \"all levels\"; Vasiliev uniform-gain in the threshold entry; APRE named in the isokinetic entry). All hold.\nDuplicates: searched the vault library and all staging libraries (including the 5b and 5c files in the same folder) for impulse-momentum, DeLorme, APRE/DAPRE, Berger, Ivanov 1966, Lelikov, analytical-synthetic, cross training, multi-faceted, Rogener, Egstrom, Zubov, thigh cuffs, Table 5.3, Krusen, Narikashvili, 6SM, \"20% of 1RM\", \"independent qualities\", \"literally copying\". No entry in scope restates an existing entry without a new angle. The Newton entry reuses Fig 5.1 (already atomized from Ch 2.2.2) but says so and adds the impulse-momentum angle.\n\nCounts: **GO 11, FIX 8, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Per-entry verdicts\n\n| file | verdict | problem (entry vs source, with page) | exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md | FIX | `proven` is correctly scoped to the impulse-momentum identity, and (a), (b), (c) are correct physics. Three physics sentences are imprecise. (1) The entry quotes the book's \"I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV\" and \"the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" without flagging that the book defines ΔV as \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase\" with the integral running from 0 (PDF 267 / p.256, image). m times a final velocity equals the change in momentum only if the body starts from rest. (2) When to apply: \"a driver metric such as propulsive impulse to the output metric (take-off velocity, hence jump height)\". Only the net impulse (vertical GRF minus body weight) fixes take-off velocity; GRF impulse alone does not. (3) When NOT (d): \"constant-speed circular motion has a constant-size force that only turns\". A force that turns is a changing force vector, so this does not counter the book's \"associated with a change in force\"; it confirms it. (c) also says \"net force on a free body\", which is loose. | (1) Add to When NOT as (e): \"Inference: the book defines ΔV as the final velocity of the phase and integrates from 0, so m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general impulse = m(v_final - v_initial).\" (2) Replace \"propulsive impulse\" with \"net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase)\". (3) In (d), replace the circular-motion example with: \"a projectile under gravity changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\" In (c), replace \"net force on a free body\" with \"net external force on the body\". Keep `proven` with its existing scope. |\n| principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md | GO | none | checked: \"totally independent qualities\", \"does not necessarily enhance\", \"(Verkhoshansky, 1963)\", \"inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4)\" at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); Example labelled Inference; baseline line 101 quote and Explicit Q1 tag; `supported` appropriate. Suggest reciprocal link to `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4, transfer weakens with qualification). |\n| heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md | GO | none | checked: analytical-synthetic definition, gymnasts/weightlifters sprints and barbell for track (Verkhoshansky 1977), \"appropriate only for the initial stages ... serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\", \"partly true and only under certain circumstances\", Yakovlev 1968 / Zimkin 1965 / Zatsiorsky 1965, \"inevitably opposes the law of gradual development\", cross training \"(see 5.5)\", \"sequentiality, simultaneity or scheme of superposition\" all at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); baseline line 182; Ch 2 counter-quote \"positively influences the development of the others\" present in the linked entry. Suggest reciprocal link to the 5.5 entry `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label`. |\n| heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md | FIX | (1) Location wrong. Entry: \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up\". The neuromuscular-system paragraph (\"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect ... requires specialised means of training\") closes the dynamics discussion: it follows Fig 5.1 at the foot of PDF 267 / p.256, and \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" opens PDF 268 / p.257 (image). (2) Unlabelled writer judgment and application. When to apply: \"This passage is the authors' own statement of that 'not surface resemblance' rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, ...)\" has no \"Inference:\" in that paragraph. | (1) Replace \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up\" with \"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1, sets this up\". (2) Prefix \"This passage is the authors' own statement ...\" with \"Inference:\". All quotes otherwise verified at PDF 268 / p.257 and PDF 277-278 / p.266-267 (image). |\n| framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type.md | GO | none | checked: four sport categories (Diachkov 1961), strength types with Diachkov / Kuznetsov / Chudinov, three principles incl. \"(Verkhoshansky, 1970)\", maximal tension / speed-strength / strength-endurance loads, \"repetitive, maximal and dynamic effort (Zatsiorsky, 1966)\", five method groups, \"(see 3.1)\" at PDF 269 / p.258 (image); section headings 5.2.1 at PDF 269, 5.2.3 at PDF 275, 5.2.4 at PDF 278, 5.2.5 at PDF 296 (text-layer search); baseline line 188. Football-as-group-4 is inside an Inference sentence. |\n| framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | FIX | `contested` is justified and all four contradiction pairs are on the cited pages (image): sets \"more than three (Berger, 1962)\" PDF 271 / p.260 vs \"reducing the number of sets and repetitions\" PDF 272 / p.261; \"larger training effect\" PDF 271 / p.260 vs \"Similar significant increases\" PDF 272 / p.261; Salter 1955 PDF 271 / p.260 and Rasch & Morehouse PDF 272 / p.261 vs the same \"similar\" sentence; 5RM \"about 89%\" PDF 269 / p.258, \"90-95% of 1RM ... (3-6)\" PDF 272 / p.261, \"85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM\" PDF 271 / p.260. Two problems. (1) Item (4) overstates: \"These three are not consistent\". The clear clash is 90-95% with up to 6 reps against 5RM = 89% (6 reps must sit below 89%). \"85-95% i.e. 3-5RM\" puts the 5RM near 85% against 89%, a loose mismatch only. (2) When to apply has a sentence fragment carrying a baseline quote: \"The baseline's off-season rule (\"relative strength and technique first; ...\", line 105, course material, Inferred).\" | (1) Replace \"These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion.\" with \"The 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%); the 85-95% = 3-5RM pairing is only loosely off (it implies a 5RM near 85%). Do not use any of them as a rep-to-load conversion.\" (2) Rewrite the fragment as: \"Eric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes 'relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work' (line 105, Inferred, transcript and slides).\" Also verified: Kabat 1947 / Hellebrant & Houtz 1956 at PDF 269 (image; text layer reads \"Rabat\"); Medvedev 1967 / Vorobyev 1971 \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3\" at PDF 271; baseline lines 61, 105, 260. |\n| claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | FIX | Unlabelled claim not on the page. When NOT: \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work, mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects.\" PDF 269-270 / p.258-259 gives no subject populations for Faulkner, McMorris & Elks or Krusen; only McMorris & Elks is qualified (\"particularly with advanced athletes\"). | Replace with: \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work; the book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\" Otherwise verified (image): 89% / 74% and the DeLorme 50%-75%-100% of 10RM (Hettinger 1961) at PDF 269 / p.258; Oxford/Zinovieff and McCloy sequences, \"add nothing new\", 154% vs 142%, 212.8% vs 186% (Faulkner 1950), 5.5%, McMorris & Elks 1954, Krusen 1949 25/50/75% vs 5RM/125%/150% at PDF 270 / p.259; Narikashvili 1960 at PDF 271 / p.260; baseline line 107. |\n| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | FIX | When NOT misstates traceability. Entry: \"conclusion 1 speaks of 'maximal weights three times per week,' which is none of the five listed programmes, and conclusions 3, 5 and 6 refer to data the page does not describe.\" Source PDF 270 / p.259 (image), Berger 1963 paragraph: \"with maximal weights (10RM for one set three times per week)\" and \"There was also no major difference between the effectiveness of 2 or 3 training sessions per week.\" So conclusion 1's comparator and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger 1963 sentences, and conclusion 3 (3RM to 10RM) is loosely backed by the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results. Only conclusion 6 has no data on the page. Minor: the separate p.259 sentence that \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters ... especially where strength-skill is involved\" is missing from the advanced-lifter contrast. | Replace the sentence with: \"The six conclusions are drawn partly from data outside the five-programme list: conclusion 1's 'maximal weights three times per week' and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger (1963) sentences ('10RM for one set three times per week'; 'no major difference between ... 2 or 3 training sessions per week', PDF 270 / p.259), conclusion 3 loosely to the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results, and conclusion 6 to no data on the page.\" Add after the Vorobyev (1971) clause: \"and 'other studies' show most weightlifting training for advanced lifters is done with 1-3 repetitions, 'emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved' (PDF 270 / p.259).\" Otherwise verified (image): 3 x 6RM (Berger 1962), Keipen 1956, Vasiliev 1954 rest, five programmes and \"only ... the fifth one\", conclusions 1-6, italic \"these conclusions were based on research with untrained subjects\", Vorobyev 1978 \"1-6\" at PDF 270-271 / p.259-260. |\n| claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 271 / p.260): Vorobyev 1978 tempo sentence; 30 vs 15 sessions; \"22.6 ± 0.6 kg; and 16.3 ± 0.5 kg\" (Lelikov 1975); Vasiliev 1954 equal work at 20/40/60/80% and 45 lifts/min; \"maximal tempo became less effective as the load was increased\"; 20% exception. Overlap with `claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus` correctly declared (that entry holds the uniform-gain point; this one adds tempo). Baseline line 181. |\n| claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md | FIX | Title contradicts the data and the entry's own body. Title: \"Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most\". Source PDF 272 / p.261 (image): there is no eccentric test; eccentric work gave the largest squat 1RM gain (15 kg), isometric the largest isometric gain (30.2 kg), and only concentric raised the vertical jump (3.7 cm). The entry's When NOT already notes \"a squat 1RM is decided by its concentric phase, yet eccentric training raised it most\". Minor: Why it works glosses the book's phrase with \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\", which the book does not say. | Retitle: \"Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\" (rename file to match, e.g. `claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md`, and update the four inbound links in this batch). In Why it works, prefix the gloss \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\" with \"Inference:\". Numbers verified (image): 15, 9.2, 30.2 (text layer \"iJO.2\"), 14.6, 3.7, -1.6, -5.4 cm; baseline line 76 and its contested tag. |\n| framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md | GO | none | checked: DAPRE (Knight 1979) sets at PDF 272 / p.261; APRE 3RM/6RM/10RM labels, 2-4 min rest, Table 5.1 every cell at PDF 273 / p.262 (image); Table 5.2 every cell at PDF 274 / p.263 (image): 6RM 0-2 decr 2.5-5, 3-4 decr 0-2.5, 5-7 same, 8-12 incr 2.5-5, 13+ incr 5-7.5; 10RM 4-6 decr 2.5-5, 7-8 decr 0-2.5, 9-11 same, 12-16 incr 2.5-5, 17+ incr 5-7.5; 3RM 1-2 decr 2.5-5, 3-4 same, 5-6 incr 2.5-5, 7+ incr 5-10. \"6-8 weeks\" and ballistic-end quote at PDF 274. `contested` stated reason holds: \"more than 6 ... must be increased\" PDF 273 / p.262 vs 5-7 \"leave same\" PDF 274 / p.263, both on the cited pages. Example labelled Inference and consistent with the table. Baseline lines 61, 130, 67. |\n| heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing.md | GO | none | checked (image): \"skills/learning phase of about two weeks\", 20%/10% bodymass, doubled in week 2, pyramid 40%/20%, 10-8-6, 3-4 s per phase, separate and together limbs, prolonged eccentric \"about double\", at PDF 273 / p.262; isokinetic alternative, PNF, 6RM average person / 3RM serious athletes / 10RM hypertrophy, girth and skinfold at PDF 275 / p.264. `untested` fits a protocol with no data. Baseline lines 61, 141. |\n| heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 274 / p.263): \"6 repetitions of 100% of one's 1RM held for 6-8 seconds\", 6SM, 5-10 s rest, 50%/75%/to-failure sets, 6RM column, angle-series advice; Fig 5.2 axes (kgf 4-14, time 2-14 s), 100% curve peaking about 13.8 kgf near 6 s, 80% near 12, caption \"Ivenov\". Fig 5.3 (PDF 275 / p.264): 1-rep curve near 8 kgf, top curves about 14-16 kgf; the entry is right that the 4/5/6-rep curves cannot be ranked with confidence. Caption \"percentages of maximal contraction\" vs text \"1RM\" mismatch correctly flagged. Baseline lines 78, 134. |\n| framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 275 / p.264): two groups, \"generally unimportant ... decisive role in the second\", group-1 rapid-reaction movements, three tension types with definitions, deferral to 5.2.4, \"transference ... relatively poor\", \"far from being thoroughly understood\". The explosive-ballistic placement oddity is real and correctly flagged. Baseline line 76. Nit (optional): the quote \"plays a decisive role in the second.\" cuts \"type of movement\" inside the quote marks. |\n| heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md | GO | none | checked (image): Yakovlev 1960, \"approximately 20% of 1RM\", citation list at PDF 275 / p.264; 146%, \"up to 40% of 1RM\", 60-80% fast isometrics, \"5:1\", \"ranked athlete\" at PDF 276 / p.265; inertia \"very seldom used\" at PDF 277 / p.266; summary \"20% of 1RM ... up to 40% ... 5 to 1\" and starting conditions at PDF 278 / p.267. Baseline lines 104, 105, 61. Nit (optional): the When NOT reference \"(line 61, Explicit, Q10)\" could carry the \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix. |\n| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md | FIX | Title, filename and When to apply state a rule the page's own data do not support. Entry: \"Small Deviations From the Competition Mass Work Better\" and \"argue for overweight and underweight implements kept close to the competition mass\". That is Bondarchuk's thesis. The page's examples (PDF 276-277 / p.265-266, image) include a 3 kg javelin training weight (Matveyev 1967), 2 kg vs 4 kg medicine balls for the water polo throw (Rogener 1961), a 0.6-0.8 kg heavy puck (Savin 1974) and 2 kg table-tennis bats; only the shot (250 g vs 500 g difference) and baseball (57 g / 184 g vs 148 g) are close to competition mass. The page's stated rule is relevance to the sport, a mass that \"does not disrupt technique\", and per-athlete selection of the heavy implement. All individual numbers in the body are correct. | Retitle: \"Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Expect One-Way Transfer\" and rename the file to match (e.g. `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md`; update inbound links from the speed-strength loading and arm-throw entries). In When to apply replace \"kept close to the competition mass\" with \"chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\". Keep the Bondarchuk quote but introduce it as \"Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; ...\". Add to When NOT: \"Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a 'stay close to competition mass' rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it.\" (Competition masses are outside-book knowledge, hence the label.) |\n| case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md | GO | none | checked (image): \"38-40%\", 3 x 3 at 80% 1RM, 10 min rest, Fig 5.4 caption \"(Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\", first effort about 42-49 cm, second about 50 to 67-69 cm, levelling after about movement 8 at PDF 276 / p.265; Table 5.3 every cell (44.0/61.0/+38.8; 0.266/0.250/-6.8; 62.0/65.0/+4.8; 2.330/2.600/+11.7; 5.550/7.430/+33.8; 1.310/1.930/+47.3) and \"(Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" at PDF 277 / p.266. Recomputations confirmed: +38.6%, -6.0%, 0.62/0.266 = 2.33, 0.65/0.250 = 2.60, F x v = 12.9 and 19.3. Bischke & Morehouse 1950 present in linked PAP entry. `untested` acceptable for a single unreported-n experiment. |\n| heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md | GO | none | checked (image): rest-interval determinants, \"0.5-1 minute\", \"favourable emotional state\", cyclic relaxation phases, \"inversely proportional\", Korobkov 1953 / Monogarov 1958, Diachkov 1961 \"approach the tempo ... and even exceed it\" at PDF 277 / p.266; \"more demanding\" water resistance and limb loads at PDF 277-278; fatigue rule and \"empirical methods\" at PDF 278 / p.267. Baseline lines 96, 97. |\n| case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 278 / p.267): power greater than running, hip-flexion gather cue, 50-100 m, 100-150 g thigh cuffs, \"experienced athletes\", Zubov; third-person Verkhoshansky correctly attributed to Siff; `untested` right for a coach's anecdote. Bondarchuk and Moyer cross-references match the linked entries. Baseline line 97. |\n\n## Missing (important ideas in PDF 266-278 no entry captures)\n\nCoverage is near complete. Two small gaps, neither needs a new entry:\n\n1. PDF 270 / p.259: \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved.\" Distinct from Vorobyev (1978) \"1-6\". Fold into the Berger entry (see its FIX).\n2. PDF 278 / p.267: the section's organising conclusion that perfecting speed-strength means combining two levers, \"the positive after-effect of preceding work\" and \"exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\", plus \"Thus, to develop speed-strength...\" summary. The pieces are in the loading entry and the arm-throw case, but neither states the two-lever summary. Optional one-sentence addition to `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-...` What it is.\n\n## Reciprocal-link suggestions\n\n- `heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training` and `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label` (5.5).\n- `principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities` and `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4).\n- `framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric` and `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size` (5.2.4).\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n\n1. Newton II: flag ΔV as final velocity (equals change only from rest); \"net propulsive impulse\"; replace circular-motion example in (d) with constant-gravity projectile; \"net external force on the body\" in (c).\n2. Dynamic correspondence: fix \"paragraph before\" to \"paragraph that closes\" the dynamics discussion; label \"This passage is the authors' own statement ... Use it when\" as Inference.\n3. Three methods: say which load-rep pairing clashes (90-95%/3-6 clearly; 85-95%/3-5RM loosely); rewrite the line-105 fragment with an \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix.\n4. DeLorme: remove or label \"mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects\".\n5. Berger: correct the traceability sentence (conclusions 1 and 5 trace to Berger 1963 on p.259; only 6 is orphaned); add the 1-3 rep advanced-lifter sentence.\n6. Ivanov: retitle and rename (eccentric raised squat 1RM most; no \"own test\" for eccentric); label the \"trains the nervous system for that mode\" gloss.\n7. Implement mass: retitle and rename away from \"stay close to competition mass\"; fix When to apply; add the far-from-competition counterexamples (labelled Inference).\n8. After renames in 6 and 7, update inbound wikilinks in this batch.\n\nNo NO-GO. Both `contested` entries keep both sides on the cited pages. The single `proven` entry keeps its scope.\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffbVkwCUqe9SZYzj7913","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"57cc88e4-7bfa-49ae-8040-6f3ea1671a05","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:11:49.257Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"57cc88e4-7bfa-49ae-8040-6f3ea1671a05","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"df346a0e-c940-4278-86a0-fb0b0ebfde8f","agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019y36rh2FbtX2LURUBbgt5B","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"0ae439fd-8c52-4932-b7ef-3fbb84897f65","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:11:49.387Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md","content":"# Review: Supertraining Ch 5a (5.1 to 5.2.3), independent falsification pass\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent of the writer). Date: 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 266-278 (printed 255-267). Every page in range was rendered to `staging/_renders/r5a_p266.png` ... `r5a_p278.png` and opened as an image. Every equation, table, figure and number cited below was read from the image, not the text layer.\nBaseline: every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` at the cited line (lines 61, 67, 73, 76, 78, 79, 96, 97, 101, 103, 104, 105, 107, 130, 134, 141, 181, 182, 188, 260). All quotes and line numbers match. Tension 16 is correctly named at line 260.\nLinks: every `[[wikilink]]` in the 19 files resolves to a file in the vault library or the staging library. Claims the entries make about linked entries were spot-checked (Bischke & Morehouse in the PAP entry; breath-holding in the isometric drawbacks entry; Moyer wearable-resistance entry \"wrong in three separate ways\"; Bondarchuk leg-to-leg 50 m \"all levels\"; Vasiliev uniform-gain in the threshold entry; APRE named in the isokinetic entry). All hold.\nDuplicates: searched the vault library and all staging libraries (including the 5b and 5c files in the same folder) for impulse-momentum, DeLorme, APRE/DAPRE, Berger, Ivanov 1966, Lelikov, analytical-synthetic, cross training, multi-faceted, Rogener, Egstrom, Zubov, thigh cuffs, Table 5.3, Krusen, Narikashvili, 6SM, \"20% of 1RM\", \"independent qualities\", \"literally copying\". No entry in scope restates an existing entry without a new angle. The Newton entry reuses Fig 5.1 (already atomized from Ch 2.2.2) but says so and adds the impulse-momentum angle.\n\nCounts: **GO 11, FIX 8, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Per-entry verdicts\n\n| file | verdict | problem (entry vs source, with page) | exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md | FIX | `proven` is correctly scoped to the impulse-momentum identity, and (a), (b), (c) are correct physics. Three physics sentences are imprecise. (1) The entry quotes the book's \"I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV\" and \"the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" without flagging that the book defines ΔV as \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase\" with the integral running from 0 (PDF 267 / p.256, image). m times a final velocity equals the change in momentum only if the body starts from rest. (2) When to apply: \"a driver metric such as propulsive impulse to the output metric (take-off velocity, hence jump height)\". Only the net impulse (vertical GRF minus body weight) fixes take-off velocity; GRF impulse alone does not. (3) When NOT (d): \"constant-speed circular motion has a constant-size force that only turns\". A force that turns is a changing force vector, so this does not counter the book's \"associated with a change in force\"; it confirms it. (c) also says \"net force on a free body\", which is loose. | (1) Add to When NOT as (e): \"Inference: the book defines ΔV as the final velocity of the phase and integrates from 0, so m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general impulse = m(v_final - v_initial).\" (2) Replace \"propulsive impulse\" with \"net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase)\". (3) In (d), replace the circular-motion example with: \"a projectile under gravity changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\" In (c), replace \"net force on a free body\" with \"net external force on the body\". Keep `proven` with its existing scope. |\n| principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md | GO | none | checked: \"totally independent qualities\", \"does not necessarily enhance\", \"(Verkhoshansky, 1963)\", \"inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4)\" at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); Example labelled Inference; baseline line 101 quote and Explicit Q1 tag; `supported` appropriate. Suggest reciprocal link to `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4, transfer weakens with qualification). |\n| heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md | GO | none | checked: analytical-synthetic definition, gymnasts/weightlifters sprints and barbell for track (Verkhoshansky 1977), \"appropriate only for the initial stages ... serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\", \"partly true and only under certain circumstances\", Yakovlev 1968 / Zimkin 1965 / Zatsiorsky 1965, \"inevitably opposes the law of gradual development\", cross training \"(see 5.5)\", \"sequentiality, simultaneity or scheme of superposition\" all at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); baseline line 182; Ch 2 counter-quote \"positively influences the development of the others\" present in the linked entry. Suggest reciprocal link to the 5.5 entry `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label`. |\n| heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md | FIX | (1) Location wrong. Entry: \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up\". The neuromuscular-system paragraph (\"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect ... requires specialised means of training\") closes the dynamics discussion: it follows Fig 5.1 at the foot of PDF 267 / p.256, and \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" opens PDF 268 / p.257 (image). (2) Unlabelled writer judgment and application. When to apply: \"This passage is the authors' own statement of that 'not surface resemblance' rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, ...)\" has no \"Inference:\" in that paragraph. | (1) Replace \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up\" with \"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1, sets this up\". (2) Prefix \"This passage is the authors' own statement ...\" with \"Inference:\". All quotes otherwise verified at PDF 268 / p.257 and PDF 277-278 / p.266-267 (image). |\n| framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type.md | GO | none | checked: four sport categories (Diachkov 1961), strength types with Diachkov / Kuznetsov / Chudinov, three principles incl. \"(Verkhoshansky, 1970)\", maximal tension / speed-strength / strength-endurance loads, \"repetitive, maximal and dynamic effort (Zatsiorsky, 1966)\", five method groups, \"(see 3.1)\" at PDF 269 / p.258 (image); section headings 5.2.1 at PDF 269, 5.2.3 at PDF 275, 5.2.4 at PDF 278, 5.2.5 at PDF 296 (text-layer search); baseline line 188. Football-as-group-4 is inside an Inference sentence. |\n| framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | FIX | `contested` is justified and all four contradiction pairs are on the cited pages (image): sets \"more than three (Berger, 1962)\" PDF 271 / p.260 vs \"reducing the number of sets and repetitions\" PDF 272 / p.261; \"larger training effect\" PDF 271 / p.260 vs \"Similar significant increases\" PDF 272 / p.261; Salter 1955 PDF 271 / p.260 and Rasch & Morehouse PDF 272 / p.261 vs the same \"similar\" sentence; 5RM \"about 89%\" PDF 269 / p.258, \"90-95% of 1RM ... (3-6)\" PDF 272 / p.261, \"85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM\" PDF 271 / p.260. Two problems. (1) Item (4) overstates: \"These three are not consistent\". The clear clash is 90-95% with up to 6 reps against 5RM = 89% (6 reps must sit below 89%). \"85-95% i.e. 3-5RM\" puts the 5RM near 85% against 89%, a loose mismatch only. (2) When to apply has a sentence fragment carrying a baseline quote: \"The baseline's off-season rule (\"relative strength and technique first; ...\", line 105, course material, Inferred).\" | (1) Replace \"These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion.\" with \"The 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%); the 85-95% = 3-5RM pairing is only loosely off (it implies a 5RM near 85%). Do not use any of them as a rep-to-load conversion.\" (2) Rewrite the fragment as: \"Eric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes 'relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work' (line 105, Inferred, transcript and slides).\" Also verified: Kabat 1947 / Hellebrant & Houtz 1956 at PDF 269 (image; text layer reads \"Rabat\"); Medvedev 1967 / Vorobyev 1971 \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3\" at PDF 271; baseline lines 61, 105, 260. |\n| claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | FIX | Unlabelled claim not on the page. When NOT: \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work, mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects.\" PDF 269-270 / p.258-259 gives no subject populations for Faulkner, McMorris & Elks or Krusen; only McMorris & Elks is qualified (\"particularly with advanced athletes\"). | Replace with: \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work; the book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\" Otherwise verified (image): 89% / 74% and the DeLorme 50%-75%-100% of 10RM (Hettinger 1961) at PDF 269 / p.258; Oxford/Zinovieff and McCloy sequences, \"add nothing new\", 154% vs 142%, 212.8% vs 186% (Faulkner 1950), 5.5%, McMorris & Elks 1954, Krusen 1949 25/50/75% vs 5RM/125%/150% at PDF 270 / p.259; Narikashvili 1960 at PDF 271 / p.260; baseline line 107. |\n| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | FIX | When NOT misstates traceability. Entry: \"conclusion 1 speaks of 'maximal weights three times per week,' which is none of the five listed programmes, and conclusions 3, 5 and 6 refer to data the page does not describe.\" Source PDF 270 / p.259 (image), Berger 1963 paragraph: \"with maximal weights (10RM for one set three times per week)\" and \"There was also no major difference between the effectiveness of 2 or 3 training sessions per week.\" So conclusion 1's comparator and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger 1963 sentences, and conclusion 3 (3RM to 10RM) is loosely backed by the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results. Only conclusion 6 has no data on the page. Minor: the separate p.259 sentence that \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters ... especially where strength-skill is involved\" is missing from the advanced-lifter contrast. | Replace the sentence with: \"The six conclusions are drawn partly from data outside the five-programme list: conclusion 1's 'maximal weights three times per week' and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger (1963) sentences ('10RM for one set three times per week'; 'no major difference between ... 2 or 3 training sessions per week', PDF 270 / p.259), conclusion 3 loosely to the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results, and conclusion 6 to no data on the page.\" Add after the Vorobyev (1971) clause: \"and 'other studies' show most weightlifting training for advanced lifters is done with 1-3 repetitions, 'emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved' (PDF 270 / p.259).\" Otherwise verified (image): 3 x 6RM (Berger 1962), Keipen 1956, Vasiliev 1954 rest, five programmes and \"only ... the fifth one\", conclusions 1-6, italic \"these conclusions were based on research with untrained subjects\", Vorobyev 1978 \"1-6\" at PDF 270-271 / p.259-260. |\n| claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 271 / p.260): Vorobyev 1978 tempo sentence; 30 vs 15 sessions; \"22.6 ± 0.6 kg; and 16.3 ± 0.5 kg\" (Lelikov 1975); Vasiliev 1954 equal work at 20/40/60/80% and 45 lifts/min; \"maximal tempo became less effective as the load was increased\"; 20% exception. Overlap with `claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus` correctly declared (that entry holds the uniform-gain point; this one adds tempo). Baseline line 181. |\n| claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md | FIX | Title contradicts the data and the entry's own body. Title: \"Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most\". Source PDF 272 / p.261 (image): there is no eccentric test; eccentric work gave the largest squat 1RM gain (15 kg), isometric the largest isometric gain (30.2 kg), and only concentric raised the vertical jump (3.7 cm). The entry's When NOT already notes \"a squat 1RM is decided by its concentric phase, yet eccentric training raised it most\". Minor: Why it works glosses the book's phrase with \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\", which the book does not say. | Retitle: \"Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\" (rename file to match, e.g. `claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md`, and update the four inbound links in this batch). In Why it works, prefix the gloss \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\" with \"Inference:\". Numbers verified (image): 15, 9.2, 30.2 (text layer \"iJO.2\"), 14.6, 3.7, -1.6, -5.4 cm; baseline line 76 and its contested tag. |\n| framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md | GO | none | checked: DAPRE (Knight 1979) sets at PDF 272 / p.261; APRE 3RM/6RM/10RM labels, 2-4 min rest, Table 5.1 every cell at PDF 273 / p.262 (image); Table 5.2 every cell at PDF 274 / p.263 (image): 6RM 0-2 decr 2.5-5, 3-4 decr 0-2.5, 5-7 same, 8-12 incr 2.5-5, 13+ incr 5-7.5; 10RM 4-6 decr 2.5-5, 7-8 decr 0-2.5, 9-11 same, 12-16 incr 2.5-5, 17+ incr 5-7.5; 3RM 1-2 decr 2.5-5, 3-4 same, 5-6 incr 2.5-5, 7+ incr 5-10. \"6-8 weeks\" and ballistic-end quote at PDF 274. `contested` stated reason holds: \"more than 6 ... must be increased\" PDF 273 / p.262 vs 5-7 \"leave same\" PDF 274 / p.263, both on the cited pages. Example labelled Inference and consistent with the table. Baseline lines 61, 130, 67. |\n| heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing.md | GO | none | checked (image): \"skills/learning phase of about two weeks\", 20%/10% bodymass, doubled in week 2, pyramid 40%/20%, 10-8-6, 3-4 s per phase, separate and together limbs, prolonged eccentric \"about double\", at PDF 273 / p.262; isokinetic alternative, PNF, 6RM average person / 3RM serious athletes / 10RM hypertrophy, girth and skinfold at PDF 275 / p.264. `untested` fits a protocol with no data. Baseline lines 61, 141. |\n| heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 274 / p.263): \"6 repetitions of 100% of one's 1RM held for 6-8 seconds\", 6SM, 5-10 s rest, 50%/75%/to-failure sets, 6RM column, angle-series advice; Fig 5.2 axes (kgf 4-14, time 2-14 s), 100% curve peaking about 13.8 kgf near 6 s, 80% near 12, caption \"Ivenov\". Fig 5.3 (PDF 275 / p.264): 1-rep curve near 8 kgf, top curves about 14-16 kgf; the entry is right that the 4/5/6-rep curves cannot be ranked with confidence. Caption \"percentages of maximal contraction\" vs text \"1RM\" mismatch correctly flagged. Baseline lines 78, 134. |\n| framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 275 / p.264): two groups, \"generally unimportant ... decisive role in the second\", group-1 rapid-reaction movements, three tension types with definitions, deferral to 5.2.4, \"transference ... relatively poor\", \"far from being thoroughly understood\". The explosive-ballistic placement oddity is real and correctly flagged. Baseline line 76. Nit (optional): the quote \"plays a decisive role in the second.\" cuts \"type of movement\" inside the quote marks. |\n| heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md | GO | none | checked (image): Yakovlev 1960, \"approximately 20% of 1RM\", citation list at PDF 275 / p.264; 146%, \"up to 40% of 1RM\", 60-80% fast isometrics, \"5:1\", \"ranked athlete\" at PDF 276 / p.265; inertia \"very seldom used\" at PDF 277 / p.266; summary \"20% of 1RM ... up to 40% ... 5 to 1\" and starting conditions at PDF 278 / p.267. Baseline lines 104, 105, 61. Nit (optional): the When NOT reference \"(line 61, Explicit, Q10)\" could carry the \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix. |\n| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md | FIX | Title, filename and When to apply state a rule the page's own data do not support. Entry: \"Small Deviations From the Competition Mass Work Better\" and \"argue for overweight and underweight implements kept close to the competition mass\". That is Bondarchuk's thesis. The page's examples (PDF 276-277 / p.265-266, image) include a 3 kg javelin training weight (Matveyev 1967), 2 kg vs 4 kg medicine balls for the water polo throw (Rogener 1961), a 0.6-0.8 kg heavy puck (Savin 1974) and 2 kg table-tennis bats; only the shot (250 g vs 500 g difference) and baseball (57 g / 184 g vs 148 g) are close to competition mass. The page's stated rule is relevance to the sport, a mass that \"does not disrupt technique\", and per-athlete selection of the heavy implement. All individual numbers in the body are correct. | Retitle: \"Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Expect One-Way Transfer\" and rename the file to match (e.g. `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md`; update inbound links from the speed-strength loading and arm-throw entries). In When to apply replace \"kept close to the competition mass\" with \"chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\". Keep the Bondarchuk quote but introduce it as \"Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; ...\". Add to When NOT: \"Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a 'stay close to competition mass' rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it.\" (Competition masses are outside-book knowledge, hence the label.) |\n| case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md | GO | none | checked (image): \"38-40%\", 3 x 3 at 80% 1RM, 10 min rest, Fig 5.4 caption \"(Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\", first effort about 42-49 cm, second about 50 to 67-69 cm, levelling after about movement 8 at PDF 276 / p.265; Table 5.3 every cell (44.0/61.0/+38.8; 0.266/0.250/-6.8; 62.0/65.0/+4.8; 2.330/2.600/+11.7; 5.550/7.430/+33.8; 1.310/1.930/+47.3) and \"(Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" at PDF 277 / p.266. Recomputations confirmed: +38.6%, -6.0%, 0.62/0.266 = 2.33, 0.65/0.250 = 2.60, F x v = 12.9 and 19.3. Bischke & Morehouse 1950 present in linked PAP entry. `untested` acceptable for a single unreported-n experiment. |\n| heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md | GO | none | checked (image): rest-interval determinants, \"0.5-1 minute\", \"favourable emotional state\", cyclic relaxation phases, \"inversely proportional\", Korobkov 1953 / Monogarov 1958, Diachkov 1961 \"approach the tempo ... and even exceed it\" at PDF 277 / p.266; \"more demanding\" water resistance and limb loads at PDF 277-278; fatigue rule and \"empirical methods\" at PDF 278 / p.267. Baseline lines 96, 97. |\n| case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 278 / p.267): power greater than running, hip-flexion gather cue, 50-100 m, 100-150 g thigh cuffs, \"experienced athletes\", Zubov; third-person Verkhoshansky correctly attributed to Siff; `untested` right for a coach's anecdote. Bondarchuk and Moyer cross-references match the linked entries. Baseline line 97. |\n\n## Missing (important ideas in PDF 266-278 no entry captures)\n\nCoverage is near complete. Two small gaps, neither needs a new entry:\n\n1. PDF 270 / p.259: \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved.\" Distinct from Vorobyev (1978) \"1-6\". Fold into the Berger entry (see its FIX).\n2. PDF 278 / p.267: the section's organising conclusion that perfecting speed-strength means combining two levers, \"the positive after-effect of preceding work\" and \"exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\", plus \"Thus, to develop speed-strength...\" summary. The pieces are in the loading entry and the arm-throw case, but neither states the two-lever summary. Optional one-sentence addition to `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-...` What it is.\n\n## Reciprocal-link suggestions\n\n- `heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training` and `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label` (5.5).\n- `principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities` and `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4).\n- `framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric` and `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size` (5.2.4).\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n\n1. Newton II: flag ΔV as final velocity (equals change only from rest); \"net propulsive impulse\"; replace circular-motion example in (d) with constant-gravity projectile; \"net external force on the body\" in (c).\n2. Dynamic correspondence: fix \"paragraph before\" to \"paragraph that closes\" the dynamics discussion; label \"This passage is the authors' own statement ... Use it when\" as Inference.\n3. Three methods: say which load-rep pairing clashes (90-95%/3-6 clearly; 85-95%/3-5RM loosely); rewrite the line-105 fragment with an \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix.\n4. DeLorme: remove or label \"mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects\".\n5. Berger: correct the traceability sentence (conclusions 1 and 5 trace to Berger 1963 on p.259; only 6 is orphaned); add the 1-3 rep advanced-lifter sentence.\n6. Ivanov: retitle and rename (eccentric raised squat 1RM most; no \"own test\" for eccentric); label the \"trains the nervous system for that mode\" gloss.\n7. Implement mass: retitle and rename away from \"stay close to competition mass\"; fix When to apply; add the far-from-competition counterexamples (labelled Inference).\n8. After renames in 6 and 7, update inbound wikilinks in this batch.\n\nNo NO-GO. Both `contested` entries keep both sides on the cited pages. The single `proven` entry keeps its scope.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"57cc88e4-7bfa-49ae-8040-6f3ea1671a05","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
