{"parentUuid":"1e481fdb-57bb-4540-9ada-66812ce52582","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"df346a0e-c940-4278-86a0-fb0b0ebfde8f","agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0153A8gYqLQiPdBLmWkAhoBk","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"880e4da8-1c19-467d-8b8d-8426a6755e03","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:12:04.333Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md","oldString":"and update the four inbound links in this batch)","newString":"and update the one inbound link, in the three-methods entry)","originalFile":null,"structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":22,"oldLines":7,"newStart":22,"newLines":7,"lines":[" | claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | FIX | Unlabelled claim not on the page. When NOT: \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work, mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects.\" PDF 269-270 / p.258-259 gives no subject populations for Faulkner, McMorris & Elks or Krusen; only McMorris & Elks is qualified (\"particularly with advanced athletes\"). | Replace with: \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work; the book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\" Otherwise verified (image): 89% / 74% and the DeLorme 50%-75%-100% of 10RM (Hettinger 1961) at PDF 269 / p.258; Oxford/Zinovieff and McCloy sequences, \"add nothing new\", 154% vs 142%, 212.8% vs 186% (Faulkner 1950), 5.5%, McMorris & Elks 1954, Krusen 1949 25/50/75% vs 5RM/125%/150% at PDF 270 / p.259; Narikashvili 1960 at PDF 271 / p.260; baseline line 107. |"," | claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | FIX | When NOT misstates traceability. Entry: \"conclusion 1 speaks of 'maximal weights three times per week,' which is none of the five listed programmes, and conclusions 3, 5 and 6 refer to data the page does not describe.\" Source PDF 270 / p.259 (image), Berger 1963 paragraph: \"with maximal weights (10RM for one set three times per week)\" and \"There was also no major difference between the effectiveness of 2 or 3 training sessions per week.\" So conclusion 1's comparator and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger 1963 sentences, and conclusion 3 (3RM to 10RM) is loosely backed by the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results. Only conclusion 6 has no data on the page. Minor: the separate p.259 sentence that \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters ... especially where strength-skill is involved\" is missing from the advanced-lifter contrast. | Replace the sentence with: \"The six conclusions are drawn partly from data outside the five-programme list: conclusion 1's 'maximal weights three times per week' and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger (1963) sentences ('10RM for one set three times per week'; 'no major difference between ... 2 or 3 training sessions per week', PDF 270 / p.259), conclusion 3 loosely to the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results, and conclusion 6 to no data on the page.\" Add after the Vorobyev (1971) clause: \"and 'other studies' show most weightlifting training for advanced lifters is done with 1-3 repetitions, 'emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved' (PDF 270 / p.259).\" Otherwise verified (image): 3 x 6RM (Berger 1962), Keipen 1956, Vasiliev 1954 rest, five programmes and \"only ... the fifth one\", conclusions 1-6, italic \"these conclusions were based on research with untrained subjects\", Vorobyev 1978 \"1-6\" at PDF 270-271 / p.259-260. |"," | claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 271 / p.260): Vorobyev 1978 tempo sentence; 30 vs 15 sessions; \"22.6 ± 0.6 kg; and 16.3 ± 0.5 kg\" (Lelikov 1975); Vasiliev 1954 equal work at 20/40/60/80% and 45 lifts/min; \"maximal tempo became less effective as the load was increased\"; 20% exception. Overlap with `claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus` correctly declared (that entry holds the uniform-gain point; this one adds tempo). Baseline line 181. |","-| claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md | FIX | Title contradicts the data and the entry's own body. Title: \"Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most\". Source PDF 272 / p.261 (image): there is no eccentric test; eccentric work gave the largest squat 1RM gain (15 kg), isometric the largest isometric gain (30.2 kg), and only concentric raised the vertical jump (3.7 cm). The entry's When NOT already notes \"a squat 1RM is decided by its concentric phase, yet eccentric training raised it most\". Minor: Why it works glosses the book's phrase with \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\", which the book does not say. | Retitle: \"Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\" (rename file to match, e.g. `claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md`, and update the four inbound links in this batch). In Why it works, prefix the gloss \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\" with \"Inference:\". Numbers verified (image): 15, 9.2, 30.2 (text layer \"iJO.2\"), 14.6, 3.7, -1.6, -5.4 cm; baseline line 76 and its contested tag. |","+| claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md | FIX | Title contradicts the data and the entry's own body. Title: \"Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most\". Source PDF 272 / p.261 (image): there is no eccentric test; eccentric work gave the largest squat 1RM gain (15 kg), isometric the largest isometric gain (30.2 kg), and only concentric raised the vertical jump (3.7 cm). The entry's When NOT already notes \"a squat 1RM is decided by its concentric phase, yet eccentric training raised it most\". Minor: Why it works glosses the book's phrase with \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\", which the book does not say. | Retitle: \"Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\" (rename file to match, e.g. `claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md`, and update the one inbound link, in the three-methods entry). In Why it works, prefix the gloss \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\" with \"Inference:\". Numbers verified (image): 15, 9.2, 30.2 (text layer \"iJO.2\"), 14.6, 3.7, -1.6, -5.4 cm; baseline line 76 and its contested tag. |"," | framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md | GO | none | checked: DAPRE (Knight 1979) sets at PDF 272 / p.261; APRE 3RM/6RM/10RM labels, 2-4 min rest, Table 5.1 every cell at PDF 273 / p.262 (image); Table 5.2 every cell at PDF 274 / p.263 (image): 6RM 0-2 decr 2.5-5, 3-4 decr 0-2.5, 5-7 same, 8-12 incr 2.5-5, 13+ incr 5-7.5; 10RM 4-6 decr 2.5-5, 7-8 decr 0-2.5, 9-11 same, 12-16 incr 2.5-5, 17+ incr 5-7.5; 3RM 1-2 decr 2.5-5, 3-4 same, 5-6 incr 2.5-5, 7+ incr 5-10. \"6-8 weeks\" and ballistic-end quote at PDF 274. `contested` stated reason holds: \"more than 6 ... must be increased\" PDF 273 / p.262 vs 5-7 \"leave same\" PDF 274 / p.263, both on the cited pages. Example labelled Inference and consistent with the table. Baseline lines 61, 130, 67. |"," | heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing.md | GO | none | checked (image): \"skills/learning phase of about two weeks\", 20%/10% bodymass, doubled in week 2, pyramid 40%/20%, 10-8-6, 3-4 s per phase, separate and together limbs, prolonged eccentric \"about double\", at PDF 273 / p.262; isokinetic alternative, PNF, 6RM average person / 3RM serious athletes / 10RM hypertrophy, girth and skinfold at PDF 275 / p.264. `untested` fits a protocol with no data. Baseline lines 61, 141. |"," | heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 274 / p.263): \"6 repetitions of 100% of one's 1RM held for 6-8 seconds\", 6SM, 5-10 s rest, 50%/75%/to-failure sets, 6RM column, angle-series advice; Fig 5.2 axes (kgf 4-14, time 2-14 s), 100% curve peaking about 13.8 kgf near 6 s, 80% near 12, caption \"Ivenov\". Fig 5.3 (PDF 275 / p.264): 1-rep curve near 8 kgf, top curves about 14-16 kgf; the entry is right that the 4/5/6-rep curves cannot be ranked with confidence. Caption \"percentages of maximal contraction\" vs text \"1RM\" mismatch correctly flagged. Baseline lines 78, 134. |"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1e481fdb-57bb-4540-9ada-66812ce52582","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
