{"parentUuid":"d903a685-4d1d-447b-90f9-a00bca5cd113","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"df346a0e-c940-4278-86a0-fb0b0ebfde8f","agentId":"a2f342f6277bc9475","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TKEtTNwKKc6XvS4ZMHTRdA","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"ae6cb50f-d493-4c7d-b39e-1d540225ae7f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:12:05.444Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md","oldString":"update inbound links from the speed-strength loading and arm-throw entries)","newString":"update the one inbound link, in the speed-strength loading entry)","originalFile":null,"structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":28,"oldLines":7,"newStart":28,"newLines":7,"lines":[" | heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 274 / p.263): \"6 repetitions of 100% of one's 1RM held for 6-8 seconds\", 6SM, 5-10 s rest, 50%/75%/to-failure sets, 6RM column, angle-series advice; Fig 5.2 axes (kgf 4-14, time 2-14 s), 100% curve peaking about 13.8 kgf near 6 s, 80% near 12, caption \"Ivenov\". Fig 5.3 (PDF 275 / p.264): 1-rep curve near 8 kgf, top curves about 14-16 kgf; the entry is right that the 4/5/6-rep curves cannot be ranked with confidence. Caption \"percentages of maximal contraction\" vs text \"1RM\" mismatch correctly flagged. Baseline lines 78, 134. |"," | framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 275 / p.264): two groups, \"generally unimportant ... decisive role in the second\", group-1 rapid-reaction movements, three tension types with definitions, deferral to 5.2.4, \"transference ... relatively poor\", \"far from being thoroughly understood\". The explosive-ballistic placement oddity is real and correctly flagged. Baseline line 76. Nit (optional): the quote \"plays a decisive role in the second.\" cuts \"type of movement\" inside the quote marks. |"," | heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md | GO | none | checked (image): Yakovlev 1960, \"approximately 20% of 1RM\", citation list at PDF 275 / p.264; 146%, \"up to 40% of 1RM\", 60-80% fast isometrics, \"5:1\", \"ranked athlete\" at PDF 276 / p.265; inertia \"very seldom used\" at PDF 277 / p.266; summary \"20% of 1RM ... up to 40% ... 5 to 1\" and starting conditions at PDF 278 / p.267. Baseline lines 104, 105, 61. Nit (optional): the When NOT reference \"(line 61, Explicit, Q10)\" could carry the \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix. |","-| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md | FIX | Title, filename and When to apply state a rule the page's own data do not support. Entry: \"Small Deviations From the Competition Mass Work Better\" and \"argue for overweight and underweight implements kept close to the competition mass\". That is Bondarchuk's thesis. The page's examples (PDF 276-277 / p.265-266, image) include a 3 kg javelin training weight (Matveyev 1967), 2 kg vs 4 kg medicine balls for the water polo throw (Rogener 1961), a 0.6-0.8 kg heavy puck (Savin 1974) and 2 kg table-tennis bats; only the shot (250 g vs 500 g difference) and baseball (57 g / 184 g vs 148 g) are close to competition mass. The page's stated rule is relevance to the sport, a mass that \"does not disrupt technique\", and per-athlete selection of the heavy implement. All individual numbers in the body are correct. | Retitle: \"Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Expect One-Way Transfer\" and rename the file to match (e.g. `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md`; update inbound links from the speed-strength loading and arm-throw entries). In When to apply replace \"kept close to the competition mass\" with \"chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\". Keep the Bondarchuk quote but introduce it as \"Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; ...\". Add to When NOT: \"Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a 'stay close to competition mass' rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it.\" (Competition masses are outside-book knowledge, hence the label.) |","+| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md | FIX | Title, filename and When to apply state a rule the page's own data do not support. Entry: \"Small Deviations From the Competition Mass Work Better\" and \"argue for overweight and underweight implements kept close to the competition mass\". That is Bondarchuk's thesis. The page's examples (PDF 276-277 / p.265-266, image) include a 3 kg javelin training weight (Matveyev 1967), 2 kg vs 4 kg medicine balls for the water polo throw (Rogener 1961), a 0.6-0.8 kg heavy puck (Savin 1974) and 2 kg table-tennis bats; only the shot (250 g vs 500 g difference) and baseball (57 g / 184 g vs 148 g) are close to competition mass. The page's stated rule is relevance to the sport, a mass that \"does not disrupt technique\", and per-athlete selection of the heavy implement. All individual numbers in the body are correct. | Retitle: \"Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Expect One-Way Transfer\" and rename the file to match (e.g. `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md`; update the one inbound link, in the speed-strength loading entry). In When to apply replace \"kept close to the competition mass\" with \"chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\". Keep the Bondarchuk quote but introduce it as \"Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; ...\". Add to When NOT: \"Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a 'stay close to competition mass' rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it.\" (Competition masses are outside-book knowledge, hence the label.) |"," | case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md | GO | none | checked (image): \"38-40%\", 3 x 3 at 80% 1RM, 10 min rest, Fig 5.4 caption \"(Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\", first effort about 42-49 cm, second about 50 to 67-69 cm, levelling after about movement 8 at PDF 276 / p.265; Table 5.3 every cell (44.0/61.0/+38.8; 0.266/0.250/-6.8; 62.0/65.0/+4.8; 2.330/2.600/+11.7; 5.550/7.430/+33.8; 1.310/1.930/+47.3) and \"(Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" at PDF 277 / p.266. Recomputations confirmed: +38.6%, -6.0%, 0.62/0.266 = 2.33, 0.65/0.250 = 2.60, F x v = 12.9 and 19.3. Bischke & Morehouse 1950 present in linked PAP entry. `untested` acceptable for a single unreported-n experiment. |"," | heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md | GO | none | checked (image): rest-interval determinants, \"0.5-1 minute\", \"favourable emotional state\", cyclic relaxation phases, \"inversely proportional\", Korobkov 1953 / Monogarov 1958, Diachkov 1961 \"approach the tempo ... and even exceed it\" at PDF 277 / p.266; \"more demanding\" water resistance and limb loads at PDF 277-278; fatigue rule and \"empirical methods\" at PDF 278 / p.267. Baseline lines 96, 97. |"," | case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 278 / p.267): power greater than running, hip-flexion gather cue, 50-100 m, 100-150 g thigh cuffs, \"experienced athletes\", Zubov; third-person Verkhoshansky correctly attributed to Siff; `untested` right for a coach's anecdote. Bondarchuk and Moyer cross-references match the linked entries. Baseline line 97. |"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d903a685-4d1d-447b-90f9-a00bca5cd113","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
